WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:05.485
&gt;&gt; My name is Debadeepta Dey.

00:00:05.486 --> 00:00:08.430
I am a researcher in
Microsoft Research AI in

00:00:08.431 --> 00:00:12.365
the adaptive systems
and interaction group.

00:00:12.366 --> 00:00:15.720
So, today, we are co-organizing

00:00:15.721 --> 00:00:19.880
this session on AI
for AI systems.

00:00:19.881 --> 00:00:21.470
Just a little bit of background,

00:00:21.471 --> 00:00:24.560
my background is actually
neither systems,

00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:28.360
but it's actually machine
learning and robotics.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:31.110
So, there is a fun anecdote
I always like to

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:34.810
say from my robotics days
at Carnegie Mellon,

00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:36.480
I was once part of
a team that was

00:00:36.481 --> 00:00:38.870
building a big
self-driving vehicle,

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:42.040
not exactly a road vehicle,
but an off-road vehicle.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.050
There were a large number of

00:00:44.051 --> 00:00:46.800
sub-teams and there was this
one sub-team which was,

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:48.875
let's call them the
rock detection team.

00:00:48.876 --> 00:00:50.875
The rock detection team decided

00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:53.560
that our aim is

00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:55.600
to build the world's
best rock detector so

00:00:55.601 --> 00:00:58.795
that self-driving cars
won't drive over them and

00:00:58.796 --> 00:01:00.730
we can avoid them and
do path planning and

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:02.530
everything pretty
reliably and robustly.

00:01:02.531 --> 00:01:04.120
So, they very quickly built up

00:01:04.121 --> 00:01:06.400
the first nominal version and

00:01:06.401 --> 00:01:08.760
this rock detector was good.

00:01:08.761 --> 00:01:11.090
It was on our test datasets,

00:01:11.091 --> 00:01:12.540
proprietary test datasets, they

00:01:12.541 --> 00:01:14.105
were like 70 percent accurate.

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:16.720
So, to not hold up the rest of

00:01:16.721 --> 00:01:17.980
all the modules that were being

00:01:17.981 --> 00:01:20.425
developed by all the other teams,

00:01:20.426 --> 00:01:21.770
they decided that, okay,

00:01:21.771 --> 00:01:23.245
we will push this out,

00:01:23.246 --> 00:01:24.705
let the team work with that,

00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:26.150
and then we will come back

00:01:26.151 --> 00:01:28.220
and we will try to improve it on

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.780
the side while the rest of

00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:32.130
the teams are using
this in the pipeline.

00:01:32.131 --> 00:01:33.590
So, six months later,

00:01:33.591 --> 00:01:35.395
they suddenly come back
and they say, "Hey,

00:01:35.396 --> 00:01:39.050
we now have a 98 percent
accuracy rock detector

00:01:39.051 --> 00:01:40.385
and it works great,

00:01:40.386 --> 00:01:42.110
and we have the same
function signatures

00:01:42.111 --> 00:01:43.290
and API interfaces,

00:01:43.291 --> 00:01:47.010
so just drag and drop this
back into the codebase,

00:01:47.011 --> 00:01:49.060
we'll do a pull request
and you're good."

00:01:49.061 --> 00:01:51.815
Right? So everybody
was very happy,

00:01:51.816 --> 00:01:54.140
and they went and they pulled

00:01:54.141 --> 00:01:57.025
from upstream and then the
system just broke down.

00:01:57.026 --> 00:02:01.280
This is a theme we kept seeing
over and over again that

00:02:01.281 --> 00:02:05.380
as each individual module
was improving locally,

00:02:05.381 --> 00:02:07.255
the entire system would degrade,

00:02:07.256 --> 00:02:09.660
and we realized that

00:02:09.661 --> 00:02:12.230
the systems that we're
downstream from it or sometimes

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.730
of an upstream from
it were learning to

00:02:13.731 --> 00:02:18.200
compensate for different
distribution of outputs.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.710
When I joined MSR, and
was in Eric's group,

00:02:22.711 --> 00:02:24.770
and Eric and me have always had

00:02:24.771 --> 00:02:26.525
this long discussions about

00:02:26.526 --> 00:02:28.730
how we build systems nominally,

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:30.600
and the systems we
just like build them,

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:33.390
we try to do our best
to optimize them,

00:02:33.391 --> 00:02:35.585
to make them as fast as possible,

00:02:35.586 --> 00:02:37.880
but our systems don't reflect,

00:02:37.881 --> 00:02:41.875
they don't adapt really and
do limited computation,

00:02:41.876 --> 00:02:44.205
the concepts of
bounded rationality,

00:02:44.206 --> 00:02:47.525
and the fact that we will
always have partial information

00:02:47.526 --> 00:02:52.110
and finite compute
especially on Edge,

00:02:52.111 --> 00:02:56.240
robotics, the new classes
of doing machine learning,

00:02:56.241 --> 00:02:58.560
serving or training as fast

00:02:58.561 --> 00:03:01.670
as possible, interactive systems.

00:03:01.671 --> 00:03:05.840
So, I feel like this session
is very timely,

00:03:05.841 --> 00:03:08.600
because just as systems has now

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:12.030
enabled a new revolution
or the new boom

00:03:12.031 --> 00:03:15.455
in AI so as to speak
and big data is now

00:03:15.456 --> 00:03:19.380
really big data and
new hardware and new chips,

00:03:19.381 --> 00:03:21.445
new systems are being enabled,

00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:24.110
there's also
this question of how do

00:03:24.111 --> 00:03:26.540
you use AI to build
better AI systems,

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:29.650
so it's like kind of
a nice self-referential loop,

00:03:29.651 --> 00:03:32.140
and hopefully, we will take off.

00:03:32.141 --> 00:03:35.000
We have an exciting lineup of

00:03:35.001 --> 00:03:36.260
speakers to talk about

00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:38.675
all sorts of themes
related to this.

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:41.635
First of all, we'll
start with Eric.

00:03:41.636 --> 00:03:44.050
Just as a brief introduction,

00:03:44.051 --> 00:03:49.700
Eric is the director of MSR AI
and he also heads all of

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:53.600
Microsoft Research and we're
very pleased to have him

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:57.600
start off and then he will be
followed by Virginia Smith,

00:03:57.601 --> 00:04:00.630
who is just starting
as faculty at CMU,

00:04:00.631 --> 00:04:03.020
talking to us about like

00:04:03.021 --> 00:04:07.130
machine learning in the Edge
and how to do Federated

00:04:07.131 --> 00:04:09.080
learning better and then
we will have Behnaz from

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:12.410
the Systems Groups at
Microsoft Research and she

00:04:12.411 --> 00:04:13.850
will tell us about how

00:04:13.851 --> 00:04:15.440
machine learning is
being used and what are

00:04:15.441 --> 00:04:18.890
the problems in
networking databases,

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.860
VMs in the cloud and

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.360
in really true big systems

00:04:23.361 --> 00:04:25.375
how to do troubleshooting better.

00:04:25.376 --> 00:04:28.470
Without much further ado, I'll
hand over the mic to Eric.

00:04:28.471 --> 00:04:28.840
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:04:28.841 --> 00:04:31.410
I think I have a mic that
I'm actually wearing.

00:04:31.411 --> 00:04:34.035
So, for many years,

00:04:34.036 --> 00:04:35.695
since my dissertation work,

00:04:35.696 --> 00:04:39.035
I've been fascinated
and intrigued

00:04:39.036 --> 00:04:43.220
at models of reflection
in AI systems.

00:04:43.221 --> 00:04:48.770
What I mean broadly by
that are representations

00:04:48.771 --> 00:04:50.600
and inference strategies
that actually

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:55.435
look at existing uncontrolled

00:04:55.436 --> 00:04:57.850
or unmoderated AI systems that

00:04:57.851 --> 00:05:01.180
come to control them
and optimize them both

00:05:01.181 --> 00:05:05.340
for different kinds of
distributional settings,

00:05:05.341 --> 00:05:09.050
shifts for example,
stream of instances,

00:05:09.051 --> 00:05:12.610
varying amounts of time
available for computation to

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.560
generalize the ability
of an agent to do

00:05:14.561 --> 00:05:16.860
well in the open world.

00:05:16.861 --> 00:05:20.530
So, thinking more deeply about
potential architectures of

00:05:20.531 --> 00:05:22.480
intelligence that might involve

00:05:22.481 --> 00:05:25.455
one or more layers of reflection,

00:05:25.456 --> 00:05:29.990
including reasoning about
reasoning or metareasoning.

00:05:29.991 --> 00:05:32.815
I thought I would just mention
four topic areas today

00:05:32.816 --> 00:05:35.780
that I think are

00:05:35.781 --> 00:05:39.514
interesting directions in
the realm of reflection.

00:05:39.515 --> 00:05:42.150
Learning to control
inferential processes

00:05:42.151 --> 00:05:44.570
given a base process,

00:05:44.571 --> 00:05:47.180
the idea of using portfolios of

00:05:47.181 --> 00:05:51.169
components to build
more robust systems,

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.780
metareasoning for
ideal allocation of

00:05:53.781 --> 00:05:57.064
resources for this idea
of being more flexible

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:00.410
to distribution of
problem instances as well as

00:06:00.411 --> 00:06:03.960
to a utility function,

00:06:03.961 --> 00:06:06.770
for example, overtime,
time criticality,

00:06:06.771 --> 00:06:08.930
and then talking a little bit
about policies and

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:11.335
predictions for
speculative execution,

00:06:11.336 --> 00:06:17.960
executing with available
resources now about the future.

00:06:17.961 --> 00:06:21.110
These are all obviously
interrelated topics.

00:06:21.111 --> 00:06:22.480
So, first on this idea of

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:27.630
learning to control
inferential processes.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.100
So for me, the canonical problem

00:06:31.101 --> 00:06:32.480
in this realm was some work we

00:06:32.481 --> 00:06:34.180
did that was published at

00:06:34.181 --> 00:06:37.290
UAI which was looking
at a theorem prover,

00:06:37.291 --> 00:06:41.430
looking at NP-Hard problems
to satisfy ability,

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.350
constraint satisfaction problems,

00:06:44.351 --> 00:06:46.275
and other related problems.

00:06:46.276 --> 00:06:49.365
We had access to
a theorem prover,

00:06:49.366 --> 00:06:51.255
several theorem provers actually,

00:06:51.256 --> 00:06:53.330
and the basic idea
was we would be given

00:06:53.331 --> 00:06:56.870
hard problems and then
we'd run the systems

00:06:56.871 --> 00:06:58.715
and the community noticed

00:06:58.716 --> 00:07:01.970
that a solution could

00:07:01.971 --> 00:07:05.130
be found very quickly
or take a very,

00:07:05.131 --> 00:07:08.910
very long time, I mean
like many, many varies.

00:07:08.911 --> 00:07:11.510
In fact, there's actually
a halting problem as

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:14.120
well with some of
these challenges,

00:07:14.121 --> 00:07:19.310
and it was unclear

00:07:19.311 --> 00:07:20.630
if we actually knew how to

00:07:20.631 --> 00:07:23.370
discriminate problems that
would go fast versus slow.

00:07:23.371 --> 00:07:25.130
On top of that, we had access to

00:07:25.131 --> 00:07:27.440
this idea of randomization of

00:07:27.441 --> 00:07:29.075
a seed that would start

00:07:29.076 --> 00:07:32.430
a new version of
an analysis of a problem.

00:07:32.431 --> 00:07:34.840
So, when you start
a theorem-proving problem,

00:07:34.841 --> 00:07:37.290
you can let the system
run and run and run.

00:07:37.291 --> 00:07:38.320
At some point in time,

00:07:38.321 --> 00:07:39.400
you can say, "You know what,

00:07:39.401 --> 00:07:41.390
I'm going to stop this
and restart it with

00:07:41.391 --> 00:07:43.800
a new randomized set of

00:07:43.801 --> 00:07:47.195
settings and try again to
try to find a solution."

00:07:47.196 --> 00:07:49.890
It was noticed heuristically by

00:07:49.891 --> 00:07:50.940
Carla Gomes actually and

00:07:50.941 --> 00:07:52.815
Bart Selman that
you could actually,

00:07:52.816 --> 00:07:58.140
by taking a whole workload
of instances and running

00:07:58.141 --> 00:07:59.780
into a theorem prover and

00:07:59.781 --> 00:08:03.440
moving a restart time
for the distribution,

00:08:03.441 --> 00:08:05.705
just like keep on moving it.

00:08:05.706 --> 00:08:10.170
When you restart, you throw
away all your investment,

00:08:10.171 --> 00:08:12.950
so you're hoping to find
a short run that will solve

00:08:12.951 --> 00:08:15.995
your problem at
the cost of many runs,

00:08:15.996 --> 00:08:18.680
and then heuristically setting
these system and you say,

00:08:18.681 --> 00:08:20.305
"Well, I wonder if we could
do better than that."

00:08:20.306 --> 00:08:22.050
So, the basic idea was we said,

00:08:22.051 --> 00:08:25.460
"What might we do from
the point of view of

00:08:25.461 --> 00:08:28.190
a machine learning
problem to basically look

00:08:28.191 --> 00:08:30.920
at this in a Bayesian
way," and we first said,

00:08:30.921 --> 00:08:32.870
let's look at the instances
and try to characterize

00:08:32.871 --> 00:08:35.915
the actual instance
by featurising it.

00:08:35.916 --> 00:08:41.315
Let's actually, insights itself,

00:08:41.316 --> 00:08:43.740
let's featurize the environment

00:08:43.741 --> 00:08:46.260
where this generator that is
generating the instances,

00:08:46.261 --> 00:08:47.820
and then when you
run this system,

00:08:47.821 --> 00:08:52.240
lets take a whole
pattern of evidence,

00:08:52.241 --> 00:08:55.805
like actually we featurized
the actual search tree.

00:08:55.806 --> 00:08:59.300
What's happening in
the initial part of the run

00:08:59.301 --> 00:09:02.640
here and how long

00:09:02.641 --> 00:09:03.680
has it been running
became a feature as

00:09:03.681 --> 00:09:05.475
well without finding an answer.

00:09:05.476 --> 00:09:08.960
So, that can be part
of your feature space.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.890
Magically, I remember that I was

00:09:11.891 --> 00:09:14.970
actually running
WinMine on an airplane,

00:09:14.971 --> 00:09:17.015
cut off from the world
in those days,

00:09:17.016 --> 00:09:19.875
with this big dataset
traveling across the country,

00:09:19.876 --> 00:09:23.270
and we discovered there
was actual rich signal

00:09:23.271 --> 00:09:25.670
in the actual evidence

00:09:25.671 --> 00:09:27.865
being spewed from
the theorem prover.

00:09:27.866 --> 00:09:31.035
You can read about all the
distinctions in the UAI paper,

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:34.995
to build a model that
would predict for any run,

00:09:34.996 --> 00:09:37.415
will this be a short or long run

00:09:37.416 --> 00:09:40.420
in advance of the run completing.

00:09:40.421 --> 00:09:42.785
Actually, this led to
a body of literature,

00:09:42.786 --> 00:09:44.180
I mean of several papers,

00:09:44.181 --> 00:09:46.310
where we showed you
could actually use

00:09:46.311 --> 00:09:49.115
machine learning to
take a very hard,

00:09:49.116 --> 00:09:51.380
sort of a NP-hard problem,

00:09:51.381 --> 00:09:54.260
and cut through
the hardness space with

00:09:54.261 --> 00:09:57.710
Bayesian inference about when
to restart and randomize

00:09:57.711 --> 00:09:58.970
and showed how you could extend

00:09:58.971 --> 00:10:05.365
the power of one of
the hardest AI problems we know.

00:10:05.366 --> 00:10:08.185
Theorem-proving.
That's one example.

00:10:08.186 --> 00:10:10.010
Another example of reflection,

00:10:10.011 --> 00:10:11.140
I think we need to
think more about as

00:10:11.141 --> 00:10:12.350
a community is how do

00:10:12.351 --> 00:10:14.810
we take portfolios of

00:10:14.811 --> 00:10:17.930
solutions and putting them

00:10:17.931 --> 00:10:19.760
together in
a coherent way to make

00:10:19.761 --> 00:10:24.260
more robust reasoners and
the canonical version of

00:10:24.261 --> 00:10:27.630
this for me is
a face-tracking problem

00:10:27.631 --> 00:10:28.820
that I worked on many,

00:10:28.821 --> 00:10:31.520
many years back, but
it actually also

00:10:31.521 --> 00:10:34.550
resonates with today's work
going on even in this room,

00:10:34.551 --> 00:10:36.410
I see best guys who work
in this space as well

00:10:36.411 --> 00:10:40.560
to understand
context-based performance.

00:10:40.561 --> 00:10:43.185
An incidence-based performance

00:10:43.186 --> 00:10:45.775
of a machine learning
problem for example.

00:10:45.776 --> 00:10:48.010
In this case, we're looking
at a face tracking here.

00:10:48.011 --> 00:10:49.870
The idea is to take successes and

00:10:49.871 --> 00:10:51.930
failures and build a
model of confidence,

00:10:51.931 --> 00:10:54.670
which is so important in AI
systems in the open world.

00:10:54.671 --> 00:10:58.430
In this case, we had

00:10:58.431 --> 00:11:00.585
an algorithm of a certain type

00:11:00.586 --> 00:11:02.890
that would actually provide us,

00:11:02.891 --> 00:11:04.440
we were interested in
the inferred state

00:11:04.441 --> 00:11:05.540
where the face was,

00:11:05.541 --> 00:11:08.640
there was the idea of ground
truth which influences that,

00:11:08.641 --> 00:11:10.050
and then we had this idea

00:11:10.051 --> 00:11:13.740
of some interesting
variable here,

00:11:13.741 --> 00:11:16.194
a very important one called
inference reliability,

00:11:16.195 --> 00:11:19.020
real-time reliability
of inference.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.730
We couldn't see this
directly in our algorithms,

00:11:22.731 --> 00:11:24.480
but we can actually view what

00:11:24.481 --> 00:11:27.170
we called reliability indicators.

00:11:27.171 --> 00:11:29.200
Each algorithm we tried had

00:11:29.201 --> 00:11:31.080
different reliability
indicators and

00:11:31.081 --> 00:11:32.475
a bit with a different model for.

00:11:32.476 --> 00:11:34.515
Now, it turns out
that we could use

00:11:34.516 --> 00:11:36.870
different kinds of
approaches in those days,

00:11:36.871 --> 00:11:39.865
color-based model, motion
decay model, we called one,

00:11:39.866 --> 00:11:42.010
this idea of
background subtraction,

00:11:42.011 --> 00:11:45.150
but the fun thing was we
built the model for each,

00:11:45.151 --> 00:11:46.555
we learn a model for
each of these things

00:11:46.556 --> 00:11:48.510
woven together and

00:11:48.511 --> 00:11:52.445
boom we had a much more
reliable face tracker.

00:11:52.446 --> 00:11:54.350
What I found most interesting in

00:11:54.351 --> 00:11:55.880
this approach for
the portfolio is,

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:57.890
there's an independently
developed algorithms, of course,

00:11:57.891 --> 00:11:58.990
that you can find dependencies of

00:11:58.991 --> 00:12:00.860
various kinds and how they work,

00:12:00.861 --> 00:12:04.030
but I was excited reading
about this in the paper,

00:12:04.031 --> 00:12:05.684
in that we were now handling

00:12:05.685 --> 00:12:08.190
unmodeled situations
in the open world.

00:12:08.191 --> 00:12:10.455
So, for example, we never
really trained the system

00:12:10.456 --> 00:12:13.280
on this problem of facing away

00:12:13.281 --> 00:12:17.635
from the camera or
a jolted camera

00:12:17.636 --> 00:12:20.630
or a peripheral distraction
of a person walking through

00:12:20.631 --> 00:12:23.550
the scene or turning
lights on and off.

00:12:23.551 --> 00:12:27.560
But, you see since these are
very different algorithms,

00:12:27.561 --> 00:12:30.240
this led to the initial
intuition as to how

00:12:30.241 --> 00:12:32.240
we built the system to
begin with this approach.

00:12:32.241 --> 00:12:33.775
You see, in different settings,

00:12:33.776 --> 00:12:35.910
one or more is fuzzing out,

00:12:35.911 --> 00:12:38.820
but the reliability
indicators are quite

00:12:38.821 --> 00:12:41.660
robust to capture
and wave, and say,

00:12:41.661 --> 00:12:42.955
"I'm not doing well right now",

00:12:42.956 --> 00:12:46.135
wait things to the inferences
from the other system,

00:12:46.136 --> 00:12:48.715
and in the end we ended
up with a robust output.

