00:35:34.081 --> 00:35:37.010
Today I'll be sharing some
of my work in developing

00:35:37.011 --> 00:35:39.105
system-aware optimization methods

00:35:39.106 --> 00:35:41.135
for the federated setting.

00:35:41.136 --> 00:35:43.470
So, the motivation for this work

00:35:43.471 --> 00:35:45.780
and for a lot of my work is

00:35:45.781 --> 00:35:47.895
thinking about
the machine learning

00:35:47.896 --> 00:35:50.220
workflow and trying to
make it more efficient,

00:35:50.221 --> 00:35:52.399
and more robust,
and more reliable.

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:55.850
So, what do I mean by
the machine learning workflow?

00:35:55.851 --> 00:35:58.975
This is a very coarse
description of the workflow.

00:35:58.976 --> 00:36:00.600
You start with your dataset and

00:36:00.601 --> 00:36:02.030
a problem that you'd
like to solve.

00:36:02.031 --> 00:36:04.300
Let's pretend it's something
with cat pictures,

00:36:04.301 --> 00:36:06.160
that tends to happen a lot.

00:36:06.161 --> 00:36:08.180
Under the hood, then you select

00:36:08.181 --> 00:36:09.650
your machine learning model.

00:36:09.651 --> 00:36:12.010
Then, to actually fit
that model to your data,

00:36:12.011 --> 00:36:14.450
you often have to select
an optimization algorithm

00:36:14.451 --> 00:36:16.465
to actually train that model.

00:36:16.466 --> 00:36:18.930
Okay. But often this workflow

00:36:18.931 --> 00:36:20.890
is not happening in isolation,

00:36:20.891 --> 00:36:24.660
it's happening together
with some system.

00:36:24.661 --> 00:36:28.520
This system and the parameters
of the system have

00:36:28.521 --> 00:36:29.955
a very large impact on

00:36:29.956 --> 00:36:31.410
the optimization
algorithm that you

00:36:31.411 --> 00:36:34.275
select to fit your
model to your data.

00:36:34.276 --> 00:36:37.930
So, one area that system
aware optimization

00:36:37.931 --> 00:36:41.715
has a great potential to
really improve things,

00:36:41.716 --> 00:36:43.580
is the federated setting.

00:36:43.581 --> 00:36:45.120
So, in Federated Learning, this

00:36:45.121 --> 00:36:46.500
is just a term that's been

00:36:46.501 --> 00:36:48.990
coined to describe the problem

00:36:48.991 --> 00:36:51.615
of training machine learning
models at the edge.

00:36:51.616 --> 00:36:55.020
So, as opposed to a traditional
Cloud-based training,

00:36:55.021 --> 00:36:57.020
where you might be storing
a model on the Cloud,

00:36:57.021 --> 00:36:58.405
and training on the Cloud,

00:36:58.406 --> 00:36:59.970
when it comes time
for prediction,

00:36:59.971 --> 00:37:01.905
you send raw data
from the devices,

00:37:01.906 --> 00:37:04.640
and then you get
the predictions from the Cloud,

00:37:04.641 --> 00:37:07.030
here we're trying to push
some of the computation

00:37:07.031 --> 00:37:09.810
and offloaded onto the
devices themselves.

00:37:09.811 --> 00:37:10.820
So, you can store that,

00:37:10.821 --> 00:37:12.350
the models on the
devices and you can also

00:37:12.351 --> 00:37:14.280
perform training at the edge.

00:37:14.281 --> 00:37:15.550
So, what that looks
like is, instead of

00:37:15.551 --> 00:37:17.180
actually shipping the raw data,

00:37:17.181 --> 00:37:19.720
you can send downstream
model updates

00:37:19.721 --> 00:37:21.785
and aggregate them via the Cloud,

00:37:21.786 --> 00:37:23.860
and then come to consensus on

00:37:23.861 --> 00:37:26.560
some global model that you
shipped back to the devices.

00:37:26.561 --> 00:37:29.540
So, this is becoming
more and more

00:37:29.541 --> 00:37:31.400
feasible as devices grow in terms

00:37:31.401 --> 00:37:33.485
of their computational
capabilities.

00:37:33.486 --> 00:37:36.100
It's a nice thing to do, because
it allows you to quickly

00:37:36.101 --> 00:37:38.855
incorporate new data that
you're seeing on the devices,

00:37:38.856 --> 00:37:41.020
it also helps to
reduce the strain on

00:37:41.021 --> 00:37:42.220
the network because
you're not having

00:37:42.221 --> 00:37:43.795
a ship this raw data,

00:37:43.796 --> 00:37:45.430
and it helps to increase

00:37:45.431 --> 00:37:47.530
the privacy guarantees
that you can provide.

00:37:47.531 --> 00:37:49.060
Again, because you're not
shipping the raw data,

00:37:49.061 --> 00:37:52.170
you're shipping these
downstream model updates.

00:37:52.171 --> 00:37:54.260
So, what are some examples of

00:37:54.261 --> 00:37:56.880
applications that fit
into this setting?

00:37:56.881 --> 00:38:00.310
You can imagine things
like language modeling

00:38:00.311 --> 00:38:04.080
for speech recognition
on mobile phones,

00:38:04.081 --> 00:38:06.445
adapting to pedestrian behavior

00:38:06.446 --> 00:38:08.825
in a fleet of
autonomous vehicles,

00:38:08.826 --> 00:38:10.630
or predicting health events like

00:38:10.631 --> 00:38:13.450
low blood sugar from
wearable devices.

00:38:13.451 --> 00:38:16.120
So, not all applications from

00:38:16.121 --> 00:38:19.395
the IoT setting fit
into this setting,

00:38:19.396 --> 00:38:22.790
but many do and
the key assumptions are

00:38:22.791 --> 00:38:24.960
that the local data that's being

00:38:24.961 --> 00:38:27.250
generated on each
device is important,

00:38:27.251 --> 00:38:29.390
potentially more so than

00:38:29.391 --> 00:38:31.510
some proxy data that
you could use if

00:38:31.511 --> 00:38:34.115
you were training this
model just in the Cloud.

00:38:34.116 --> 00:38:36.480
For the problems that
we're looking at,

00:38:36.481 --> 00:38:38.880
we're looking at
supervised problems where

00:38:38.881 --> 00:38:41.660
labels need to be available,

00:38:41.661 --> 00:38:44.830
and then another reason why

00:38:44.831 --> 00:38:46.840
these types of problems

00:38:46.841 --> 00:38:48.390
are becoming more and
more interesting,

00:38:48.391 --> 00:38:51.295
is that privacy is often
a very important concern,

00:38:51.296 --> 00:38:54.630
and so not having to
ship that raw data for

00:38:54.631 --> 00:38:56.940
applications like this helps

00:38:56.941 --> 00:38:59.460
to enable privacy guarantees.

00:38:59.461 --> 00:39:01.490
Okay.

00:39:01.491 --> 00:39:02.990
So what makes
this problem difficult?

00:39:02.991 --> 00:39:04.360
Why is it difficult to solve

00:39:04.361 --> 00:39:06.940
optimization problems in
the federated setting?

00:39:06.941 --> 00:39:09.595
In particular, why is it that we

00:39:09.596 --> 00:39:11.200
can't use traditional distributed

00:39:11.201 --> 00:39:12.340
optimization methods that have

00:39:12.341 --> 00:39:14.770
been developed over
the past decade and

00:39:14.771 --> 00:39:16.480
more that have been

00:39:16.481 --> 00:39:18.860
developed for the data
center setting?

00:39:18.861 --> 00:39:22.910
The issue is that traditional
data center distribute

00:39:22.911 --> 00:39:26.270
optimization methods face a suite

00:39:26.271 --> 00:39:29.710
of new challenges when looking
at the federated setting.

00:39:29.711 --> 00:39:32.490
So in particular, in the
federated setting communication

00:39:32.491 --> 00:39:35.140
can be more expensive than
in a data center setting.

00:39:35.141 --> 00:39:36.760
So, you might have rather

00:39:36.761 --> 00:39:38.690
than hundreds to
thousands of machines,

00:39:38.691 --> 00:39:40.740
you might have thousands
to millions of devices in

00:39:40.741 --> 00:39:42.620
the network and
the network itself

00:39:42.621 --> 00:39:44.810
can be slower than
a traditional data center,

00:39:44.811 --> 00:39:47.210
so you can imagine
a mobile phone network

00:39:47.211 --> 00:39:48.130
where some of the phones have

00:39:48.131 --> 00:39:50.295
very poor network connection.

00:39:50.296 --> 00:39:52.490
Additionally, there's
a huge amount of

00:39:52.491 --> 00:39:54.890
variability in the
network itself,

00:39:54.891 --> 00:39:58.680
so you might have as
compared to a data center

00:39:58.681 --> 00:40:00.440
setting where you might
assume that most of

00:40:00.441 --> 00:40:02.925
the machines have
similar computational power,

00:40:02.926 --> 00:40:05.660
here you could have
some devices that have

00:40:05.661 --> 00:40:07.870
very different computational
abilities than others.

00:40:07.871 --> 00:40:09.370
You might have certain
devices that are very

00:40:09.371 --> 00:40:11.750
old versus very new devices,

00:40:11.751 --> 00:40:14.345
some that have
very low battery power,

00:40:14.346 --> 00:40:16.250
poor network connection and

00:40:16.251 --> 00:40:17.870
then these things
can really affect

00:40:17.871 --> 00:40:19.790
strugglers in the system and can

00:40:19.791 --> 00:40:22.835
also affect issues
like fault-tolerance.

00:40:22.836 --> 00:40:25.240
So at any given
around in a network

00:40:25.241 --> 00:40:27.810
with a million devices
you can imagine that many

00:40:27.811 --> 00:40:29.020
of them if you're trying to do

00:40:29.021 --> 00:40:30.530
some sort of computation are

00:40:30.531 --> 00:40:33.495
offline or trying to do
some other sort of computation.

00:40:33.496 --> 00:40:36.580
You can't count on them for
computation every round,

00:40:36.581 --> 00:40:39.135
so fault tolerance is
a big issue in the setting.

00:40:39.136 --> 00:40:41.390
And I'll note that
this heterogeneity,

00:40:41.391 --> 00:40:43.555
this variability
comes not only from

00:40:43.556 --> 00:40:45.340
the system's perspective but

00:40:45.341 --> 00:40:47.535
also from the statistical
point of view.

00:40:47.536 --> 00:40:49.360
The issue here is
that we're generating

00:40:49.361 --> 00:40:51.120
data on these devices
and we're also trying

00:40:51.121 --> 00:40:52.330
to store and process that

00:40:52.331 --> 00:40:54.640
data and so some devices
might generate

00:40:54.641 --> 00:40:56.580
much more data than
the others and this can

00:40:56.581 --> 00:40:59.970
lead to very unbalanced data
across the network.

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:02.945
So, the statistical unbalance

00:41:02.946 --> 00:41:04.860
presents issues in terms
of how we actually

00:41:04.861 --> 00:41:06.990
solve this optimization
problem but it

00:41:06.991 --> 00:41:08.180
also presents issues in terms

00:41:08.181 --> 00:41:09.735
of how we model the problem,

00:41:09.736 --> 00:41:11.470
some particular
personalization is

00:41:11.471 --> 00:41:13.520
very key in this setting.

00:41:13.521 --> 00:41:16.360
The data that's being generated
on each of these devices,

00:41:16.361 --> 00:41:17.390
can be generated in a very

00:41:17.391 --> 00:41:19.265
non identically
distributed manner.

00:41:19.266 --> 00:41:20.740
So you might have
certain devices that

00:41:20.741 --> 00:41:22.805
generate data that's very
different from the others.

00:41:22.806 --> 00:41:25.130
For example, if we're
thinking about trying

00:41:25.131 --> 00:41:28.180
to develop voice
recognition models,

00:41:28.181 --> 00:41:30.640
my voice recognition model
may be very different

00:41:30.641 --> 00:41:31.580
than someone else's because we're

00:41:31.581 --> 00:41:33.840
generating different
sorts of data.

00:41:33.841 --> 00:41:37.500
So in the talk today,
I'm going to talk about

00:41:37.501 --> 00:41:39.640
two methods that we've developed

00:41:39.641 --> 00:41:42.340
recently to try to handle
some of these challenges,

00:41:42.341 --> 00:41:44.340
one is FedCoCoA which is

00:41:44.341 --> 00:41:47.020
a method for handling some
of the systems challenges in

00:41:47.021 --> 00:41:48.990
the federated setting
and the other

00:41:48.991 --> 00:41:50.990
is MOCHA which is something that

00:41:50.991 --> 00:41:52.860
builds on on these ideas

00:41:52.861 --> 00:41:56.280
to also allow for
personalization.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:42:01.830
So first, in terms of developing

00:42:01.831 --> 00:42:03.500
a distributed optimization method

00:42:03.501 --> 00:42:05.065
for the federated setting,

00:42:05.066 --> 00:42:07.960
again, what is the issue
with using something like

00:42:07.961 --> 00:42:09.490
a traditional data center

00:42:09.491 --> 00:42:11.745
distributed optimization method?

00:42:11.746 --> 00:42:14.300
Well, this is a question
that I ask myself because

00:42:14.301 --> 00:42:17.160
this is what I spend a lot
of my thesis working on.

00:42:17.161 --> 00:42:20.300
So for example if you look at

00:42:20.301 --> 00:42:22.750
some results from
the data center setting

00:42:22.751 --> 00:42:25.510
with state of the art methods,

00:42:25.511 --> 00:42:27.345
you'll see results like this.

00:42:27.346 --> 00:42:30.860
So on the x axis
here I'll be showing

00:42:30.861 --> 00:42:32.250
the time in seconds and on

00:42:32.251 --> 00:42:34.495
the y-axis the distance
to the optimal,

00:42:34.496 --> 00:42:36.325
so the goal is to get down to

00:42:36.326 --> 00:42:39.050
the bottom of this plot
as quickly as possible.

00:42:39.051 --> 00:42:41.460
What you see in the data center,

00:42:41.461 --> 00:42:43.320
is that if you compare

00:42:43.321 --> 00:42:45.260
a method that have
been developed in tune

00:42:45.261 --> 00:42:46.700
for this data
center settings such as

00:42:46.701 --> 00:42:49.770
CoCoA which was the focus
of my thesis work,

00:42:49.771 --> 00:42:52.520
you can get these massive
improvements over

00:42:52.521 --> 00:42:53.870
other traditional methods like

00:42:53.871 --> 00:42:55.540
mini-batch Stochastic
gradient descent

00:42:55.541 --> 00:42:57.565
or mini-batch coordinate descent.

00:42:57.566 --> 00:43:00.370
So for example, for this problem

00:43:00.371 --> 00:43:02.420
in this cluster this
was a 40th speedup,

00:43:02.421 --> 00:43:04.080
it meant that the
training time went from

00:43:04.081 --> 00:43:07.015
two hours down to
just roughly three minutes,

00:43:07.016 --> 00:43:09.280
so this makes a big impact in

00:43:09.281 --> 00:43:11.590
terms of your overall
training expense.

00:43:11.591 --> 00:43:14.935
So these methods work well
on the data center setting,

00:43:14.936 --> 00:43:16.565
let's see what happens
though when we when we

00:43:16.566 --> 00:43:19.850
apply CoCoA to
the federated setting.

00:43:19.851 --> 00:43:22.070
So what you see is in terms of

00:43:22.071 --> 00:43:24.545
the time in seconds here
to get to the optimal,

00:43:24.546 --> 00:43:25.930
CoCoA is not really
doing that well

00:43:25.931 --> 00:43:27.610
and in particular it's not doing

00:43:27.611 --> 00:43:32.020
much better than
these other baseline methods.

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:34.890
The reason for this
is that there are

00:43:34.891 --> 00:43:37.110
these additional constraints
and challenges of

00:43:37.111 --> 00:43:39.130
the federated setting that
have not been well modeled

00:43:39.131 --> 00:43:41.310
by this optimization method.

00:43:41.311 --> 00:43:43.500
So what we'll show you today is

00:43:43.501 --> 00:43:46.170
basically how to
improve upon this

00:43:46.171 --> 00:43:48.180
with this method that we're
calling fedCoCoA to get

00:43:48.181 --> 00:43:52.690
these very large
performance improvements.

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:56.995
So, I'm going to backtrack
a little bit now and talk

00:43:56.996 --> 00:43:59.900
about how CoCoA works

00:43:59.901 --> 00:44:02.255
to show you where
it's falling short,

00:44:02.256 --> 00:44:03.835
which allows us to develop

00:44:03.836 --> 00:44:06.975
this more tailored method
for the federated setting.

00:44:06.976 --> 00:44:10.110
So the motivation for CoCoA comes

00:44:10.111 --> 00:44:12.550
from the fact that there
are two sort of extremes

00:44:12.551 --> 00:44:14.710
currently and thinking about how

00:44:14.711 --> 00:44:17.740
to perform optimization
in a distributed setting.

00:44:17.741 --> 00:44:21.215
So, the goal again is that
you have a bunch of data

00:44:21.216 --> 00:44:24.630
that's distributed on
different machines or devices.

00:44:24.631 --> 00:44:26.070
So imagine each of these dark

00:44:26.071 --> 00:44:27.670
gray rectangles is a data point

00:44:27.671 --> 00:44:29.570
and each of these
light gray squares

00:44:29.571 --> 00:44:31.645
is either a device or a machine.

00:44:31.646 --> 00:44:33.990
The goal is to process all of

00:44:33.991 --> 00:44:36.310
this data and to try to
find the optimal machine

00:44:36.311 --> 00:44:37.910
learning model knowing
that you have to

00:44:37.911 --> 00:44:39.060
communicate back and forth with

00:44:39.061 --> 00:44:40.800
each of these devices
at each round.

00:44:40.801 --> 00:44:43.220
So how can we perform
this communication?

00:44:43.221 --> 00:44:44.760
On one extreme, you could take

00:44:44.761 --> 00:44:46.550
something basically
that works well

00:44:46.551 --> 00:44:48.660
on a single machine
and try to sort of

00:44:48.661 --> 00:44:51.020
naively parallelize it into
the distributed setting.

00:44:51.021 --> 00:44:52.260
This would be something like

00:44:52.261 --> 00:44:53.730
a traditional mini-batch method,

00:44:53.731 --> 00:44:56.355
mini-batch stochastic
gradient descent for example.

00:44:56.356 --> 00:44:58.845
This method works
by at each round

00:44:58.846 --> 00:45:01.110
selecting a data point
or a subset of

00:45:01.111 --> 00:45:03.240
data points at
random and computing

00:45:03.241 --> 00:45:05.695
something like a gradient
from those data points,

00:45:05.696 --> 00:45:08.045
sending those updates
back to the master,

00:45:08.046 --> 00:45:10.350
applying them to the
model and iterating

00:45:10.351 --> 00:45:11.560
doing this over and over again

00:45:11.561 --> 00:45:13.475
until convergence is reached.

00:45:13.476 --> 00:45:17.160
So these methods are
very popular and

00:45:17.161 --> 00:45:18.190
they're nice because we have

00:45:18.191 --> 00:45:20.520
nice convergence guarantees
about these methods,

00:45:20.521 --> 00:45:21.920
but in general they can be very

00:45:21.921 --> 00:45:24.280
slow in the distributed setting
because you'll be

00:45:24.281 --> 00:45:26.520
communicating very
frequently between

00:45:26.521 --> 00:45:29.030
the machines and the master.

00:45:29.031 --> 00:45:30.980
So what else is there?

00:45:30.981 --> 00:45:32.755
On the other extreme,

00:45:32.756 --> 00:45:36.650
there are methods known as
one-shot communication schemes

00:45:36.651 --> 00:45:39.410
where instead of processing
one data point or a few,

00:45:39.411 --> 00:45:41.865
you process all of the data
points completely,

00:45:41.866 --> 00:45:43.960
you completely solve
separate models on each of

00:45:43.961 --> 00:45:45.390
the machines and then you

00:45:45.391 --> 00:45:47.050
do something like
average them at the end.

00:45:47.051 --> 00:45:48.870
So, this is the other extreme.

00:45:48.871 --> 00:45:50.090
It says never communicate only

00:45:50.091 --> 00:45:51.680
communicate once at the end.

00:45:51.681 --> 00:45:53.310
It's great because it's really

00:45:53.311 --> 00:45:56.095
fast but in general convergence
is not guaranteed.

00:45:56.096 --> 00:45:58.620
So you don't have the same
nice theoretical properties

00:45:58.621 --> 00:46:01.200
that you do from
the mini-batch methods.

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:04.770
And putting this together,

00:46:04.771 --> 00:46:06.025
the nice thing about CoCoA,

00:46:06.026 --> 00:46:07.710
the reason that it is

00:46:07.711 --> 00:46:09.820
a great method to use and
the data center setting,

00:46:09.821 --> 00:46:13.465
is that it helps you to go
between these two extremes.

00:46:13.466 --> 00:46:15.850
So in particular, CoCoA takes

00:46:15.851 --> 00:46:18.320
this one system parameter
of how much you do

00:46:18.321 --> 00:46:20.950
local computation
versus communication

00:46:20.951 --> 00:46:22.720
and it exposes it to make it as

00:46:22.721 --> 00:46:24.640
flexible as possible
so that you can tune

00:46:24.641 --> 00:46:28.590
this amount of local computation
versus communication.

00:46:28.790 --> 00:46:31.860
How is CoCoA actually doing this?

00:46:31.861 --> 00:46:33.880
So the method is
actually very simple,

00:46:33.881 --> 00:46:36.220
basically what it
does is that the goal

00:46:36.221 --> 00:46:38.995
is again to find some model
across all of the data.

00:46:38.996 --> 00:46:40.550
How can we do this? We can take

00:46:40.551 --> 00:46:43.900
this global problem and
split it into subproblems,

00:46:43.901 --> 00:46:45.365
one for each machine.

00:46:45.366 --> 00:46:47.270
So each machine is trying to find

00:46:47.271 --> 00:46:49.980
some solution to
its own local subproblem.

00:46:49.981 --> 00:46:51.880
It tries to find some updates to

00:46:51.881 --> 00:46:55.125
that subproblem sends those
updates back to the master,

00:46:55.126 --> 00:46:57.240
applies that to the model and

00:46:57.241 --> 00:46:59.685
then iterates doing this
over and over again.

00:46:59.686 --> 00:47:02.335
But the thing that makes
CoCoA really flexible,

00:47:02.336 --> 00:47:03.830
is that in performing

00:47:03.831 --> 00:47:06.480
these updates to
the local subproblems,

00:47:06.481 --> 00:47:08.455
it makes the
following assumption.

00:47:08.456 --> 00:47:10.670
It assumes that each
of these sub-problems

00:47:10.671 --> 00:47:12.755
is solved to accuracy theta.

00:47:12.756 --> 00:47:15.480
So this theta parameter
is really flexible

00:47:15.481 --> 00:47:17.040
because theta is
just a number that

00:47:17.041 --> 00:47:18.890
can go between zero and one,

00:47:18.891 --> 00:47:20.450
when theta is equal to zero,

00:47:20.451 --> 00:47:21.970
it means that all
of these problems

00:47:21.971 --> 00:47:23.450
have been exactly solved on

00:47:23.451 --> 00:47:26.470
each device whereas if
theta is closer to one,

00:47:26.471 --> 00:47:29.090
it means that you are in
exactly solving the solution,

00:47:29.091 --> 00:47:32.315
perhaps you're just doing
one or two updates locally.

00:47:32.316 --> 00:47:34.020
So by tuning this parameter,

00:47:34.021 --> 00:47:35.580
you can tune roughly
the amount of

00:47:35.581 --> 00:47:38.640
local computation
versus communication.

00:47:38.641 --> 00:47:42.200
So, this seems like
a great setup to use in

00:47:42.201 --> 00:47:43.660
the federated setting
where you have

00:47:43.661 --> 00:47:46.240
this issue of
expensive communication.

00:47:46.241 --> 00:47:49.800
However, there's one issue
with this theta parameter,

00:47:49.801 --> 00:47:51.920
which is that theta
is not truly that

00:47:51.921 --> 00:47:53.330
flexible and that
you have to set it

00:47:53.331 --> 00:47:54.770
once for the entire method,

00:47:54.771 --> 00:47:56.900
so this theta parameter
is consistent

00:47:56.901 --> 00:47:59.745
across devices and
across iteration's.

00:47:59.746 --> 00:48:01.715
The reason that this
is such an issue,

00:48:01.716 --> 00:48:04.210
again is because
we have issues in

00:48:04.211 --> 00:48:07.075
the federated setting with
statistical heterogeneity.

00:48:07.076 --> 00:48:09.060
Some of the subproblems might
be harder to solve than

00:48:09.061 --> 00:48:11.735
others which would affect
this theta parameter.

00:48:11.736 --> 00:48:14.080
You might also have issues with

00:48:14.081 --> 00:48:17.300
systems heterogeneity so some
of the devices might not

00:48:17.301 --> 00:48:19.880
be able to compute good
solutions to their subproblems

00:48:19.881 --> 00:48:22.050
because they're not connected

00:48:22.051 --> 00:48:23.890
to the network they
have low power,

00:48:23.891 --> 00:48:25.910
low battery level or just

00:48:25.911 --> 00:48:26.980
their hardware is worse than

00:48:26.981 --> 00:48:29.075
the other devices in the network.

00:48:29.076 --> 00:48:31.390
And finally you can
also have issues with

00:48:31.391 --> 00:48:33.400
devices actually just
dropping off and going

00:48:33.401 --> 00:48:37.510
offline and that's
not accounted for in

00:48:37.511 --> 00:48:40.050
the original CoCoA
method because in CoCoA

00:48:40.051 --> 00:48:42.930
we don't allow this parameter
to exactly equal one,

00:48:42.931 --> 00:48:45.840
meaning that you have
to send some update.

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:50.140
So, what do we do it in
federated CoCoA to handle this?

00:48:50.141 --> 00:48:52.240
Physically, we make
this simple change.

00:48:52.241 --> 00:48:53.910
So we change theta to
be something that you

00:48:53.911 --> 00:48:56.220
can vary both by iteration and

00:48:56.221 --> 00:49:00.180
by device and this looks
deceptively simple

00:49:00.181 --> 00:49:02.700
but what becomes difficult
is actually showing

00:49:02.701 --> 00:49:04.100
that the method
can still converge

00:49:04.101 --> 00:49:05.535
given that you've done this.

00:49:05.536 --> 00:49:07.495
What we're able to show is that,

00:49:07.496 --> 00:49:10.245
using this much more
flexible accuracy parameter,

00:49:10.246 --> 00:49:12.420
with the simple assumption that

00:49:12.421 --> 00:49:14.865
the same device does

00:49:14.866 --> 00:49:17.520
not send information
every single round,

00:49:17.521 --> 00:49:19.630
so you'd have to eventually
get an update from one of

00:49:19.631 --> 00:49:21.490
the devices otherwise
you won't be able

00:49:21.491 --> 00:49:23.694
to reach the optimum.

00:49:23.695 --> 00:49:25.880
We can show two convergence

00:49:25.881 --> 00:49:28.630
guarantees one for
Lipschitz losses,

00:49:28.631 --> 00:49:30.340
this is something like

00:49:30.341 --> 00:49:31.820
the support vector machine
model you get

00:49:31.821 --> 00:49:33.970
a one over epsilon rate
of convergence

00:49:33.971 --> 00:49:36.840
and you can get a slightly
faster rate of convergence for

00:49:36.841 --> 00:49:38.270
smooth losses things like

00:49:38.271 --> 00:49:41.240
logistic regression
or linear regression.

00:49:42.010 --> 00:49:44.700
Okay, so this is
nice because now we

00:49:44.701 --> 00:49:46.670
have this setup that is much more

00:49:46.671 --> 00:49:48.060
flexible and more tailored to

00:49:48.061 --> 00:49:49.690
the federated setting and we

00:49:49.691 --> 00:49:53.160
have nice theoretical
convergence guarantees about it,

00:49:53.161 --> 00:49:55.045
but how does it
perform in practice?

00:49:55.046 --> 00:49:57.210
So what we've done
is, we've gathered

00:49:57.211 --> 00:49:59.210
some real federated
datasets from things

00:49:59.211 --> 00:50:02.215
like sensor network,
Google Glass data,

00:50:02.216 --> 00:50:04.890
human activity recognition
on mobile phones,

00:50:04.891 --> 00:50:06.700
and you can see what's
interesting is that

00:50:06.701 --> 00:50:08.440
these real federate datasets

00:50:08.441 --> 00:50:10.245
really are quite heterogeneous.

00:50:10.246 --> 00:50:12.480
So you can see that the
minimum and maximum number of

00:50:12.481 --> 00:50:16.365
data points per device really
does change significantly.

00:50:16.366 --> 00:50:18.230
For these datasets, I'll just

00:50:18.231 --> 00:50:20.375
show one example of the results.

00:50:20.376 --> 00:50:23.010
You can see that federated
CoCoA in terms of

00:50:23.011 --> 00:50:25.220
the time in seconds
is able to converge

00:50:25.221 --> 00:50:28.670
much faster than
traditional CoCoA and

00:50:28.671 --> 00:50:32.440
these very popular
mini-batch style methods

00:50:32.441 --> 00:50:35.015
which are used frequently
in the distributed setting.

00:50:35.016 --> 00:50:36.460
So in particular for this

00:50:36.461 --> 00:50:37.810
problem that we were looking at,

00:50:37.811 --> 00:50:39.390
we're able to converge to

00:50:39.391 --> 00:50:42.475
the same epsilon
accurate solution,

00:50:42.476 --> 00:50:45.370
two orders of magnitude
more quickly.

00:50:46.020 --> 00:50:49.340
What was interesting is
that we also got to look

00:50:49.341 --> 00:50:51.790
at what would happen

00:50:51.791 --> 00:50:53.640
if we just drop devices
out of the network.

00:50:53.641 --> 00:50:54.670
How would that change

00:50:54.671 --> 00:50:57.630
the performance of the
method and what you can see

00:50:57.631 --> 00:50:59.185
is that as long as we don't

00:50:59.186 --> 00:51:01.670
drop out one device
every single round,

00:51:01.671 --> 00:51:04.155
which is what is shown in
this green dotted line,

00:51:04.156 --> 00:51:06.890
the performance of
federated CoCoA is

00:51:06.891 --> 00:51:10.620
fairly robust to this issue
of devices dropping out.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:15.730
Okay. So, I want to
talk briefly as well

00:51:15.731 --> 00:51:19.640
about what we've done to
handle personalization.

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:23.075
So, going back to this,

00:51:23.076 --> 00:51:25.230
this workflow one thing about

00:51:25.231 --> 00:51:27.450
the federated setting
is that it's really

00:51:27.451 --> 00:51:29.010
impacting not only how we solve

00:51:29.011 --> 00:51:30.280
this optimization problem but

00:51:30.281 --> 00:51:32.845
also how we model
the data itself.

00:51:32.846 --> 00:51:36.945
Okay. So thinking about how
to model federated data,

00:51:36.946 --> 00:51:39.885
two extremes are, you
could imagine just

00:51:39.886 --> 00:51:41.720
selecting a separate model

00:51:41.721 --> 00:51:44.340
for each of the devices
in the network.

00:51:44.690 --> 00:51:47.230
This is nice because
it allows you to

00:51:47.231 --> 00:51:49.845
personalize the local data
that you've collected,

00:51:49.846 --> 00:51:51.640
but it's not great and
that you can't really

00:51:51.641 --> 00:51:53.795
learn from the other devices
in the network.

00:51:53.796 --> 00:51:55.070
If you're learning
a separate model

00:51:55.071 --> 00:51:55.900
for each of the devices,

00:51:55.901 --> 00:51:57.140
then you're not able to aggregate

00:51:57.141 --> 00:52:00.155
the information and learn
across the devices.

00:52:00.156 --> 00:52:01.940
On the other extreme,
if you learn

00:52:01.941 --> 00:52:05.059
just a single model
for all the devices,

00:52:05.060 --> 00:52:07.990
you're helping to learn
from the peers in

00:52:07.991 --> 00:52:09.240
the network but you're not really

00:52:09.241 --> 00:52:11.485
personalizing the
information that you have.

00:52:11.486 --> 00:52:13.230
So what we want is
something in between.

00:52:13.231 --> 00:52:14.510
We want to be able to personalize

00:52:14.511 --> 00:52:16.400
our models but also learn from

00:52:16.401 --> 00:52:17.890
the aggregate
information and boost

00:52:17.891 --> 00:52:20.685
the effective sample size
of each of the devices.

00:52:20.686 --> 00:52:23.050
So, how are we doing this?

00:52:23.051 --> 00:52:25.350
One nice way to set
this problem up is

00:52:25.351 --> 00:52:27.170
through something called
multi-task learning.

00:52:27.171 --> 00:52:30.920
So in multitask learning
you do exactly this.

00:52:30.921 --> 00:52:32.130
Basically, you're trying to find

00:52:32.131 --> 00:52:35.500
some local model for each
of the local datasets.

00:52:35.680 --> 00:52:39.180
These local models
are all captured in

00:52:39.181 --> 00:52:42.090
this aggregate matrix
W. Then what

00:52:42.091 --> 00:52:44.135
the objective is
doing is basically

00:52:44.136 --> 00:52:48.030
enforcing that these models
follow some structure.

00:52:48.031 --> 00:52:49.450
Restructure is enforced to

00:52:49.451 --> 00:52:51.850
this task relationship
matrix Omega.

00:52:51.851 --> 00:52:53.970
So the reason that this is
really nice is that you're

00:52:53.971 --> 00:52:56.260
trading off between
trying to find

00:52:56.261 --> 00:52:58.500
the optimal models for each
of the devices as well

00:52:58.501 --> 00:53:00.980
as enforcing some structure
across those models.

00:53:00.981 --> 00:53:03.410
So this allows you to have
personalized models that

00:53:03.411 --> 00:53:06.460
also share and learn
the structure across them.

00:53:06.461 --> 00:53:08.940
So this set-up for
example can capture

00:53:08.941 --> 00:53:11.545
things like all of
the tasks being related,

00:53:11.546 --> 00:53:14.725
most of them being related but
there being some outliers,

00:53:14.726 --> 00:53:16.490
clusters or groups, or

00:53:16.491 --> 00:53:18.635
things like asymmetric
relationships.

00:53:18.636 --> 00:53:21.520
For example you might have
a power user in the system

00:53:21.521 --> 00:53:24.975
that is performing most of
the interesting information.

00:53:24.976 --> 00:53:28.380
So you can capture relationships
like that as well.

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:32.920
Okay. We looked at using

00:53:32.921 --> 00:53:34.320
a multi-task set up with

00:53:34.321 --> 00:53:36.210
the real federated
datasets that we've

00:53:36.211 --> 00:53:38.180
collected and what we saw

00:53:38.181 --> 00:53:40.210
is that the multi-task
set up compared to

00:53:40.211 --> 00:53:44.885
the completely global or
completely local models

00:53:44.886 --> 00:53:47.410
consistently got
better prediction error

00:53:47.411 --> 00:53:49.305
in terms of the problems
that we were looking at.

00:53:49.306 --> 00:53:51.450
So you can see
this right-hand column,

00:53:51.451 --> 00:53:53.420
each of these prediction
error numbers

00:53:53.421 --> 00:53:56.170
are significantly lower
than the other two models,

00:53:56.171 --> 00:53:59.730
and this makes sense because
the multitask setup is

00:53:59.731 --> 00:54:01.360
really able to go between

00:54:01.361 --> 00:54:02.850
these two extremes
of the completely

00:54:02.851 --> 00:54:05.520
global or completely local model.

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:09.920
So, with this in mind,

00:54:09.921 --> 00:54:11.410
the key question then was,

00:54:11.411 --> 00:54:14.415
how can we take this nice
multi-task learning framework

00:54:14.416 --> 00:54:16.390
and apply it to some of

00:54:16.391 --> 00:54:18.385
the federated CoCoA work
that we had,

00:54:18.386 --> 00:54:20.160
that is looking at
trying to actually solve

00:54:20.161 --> 00:54:22.470
this problem in
the federated setting?

00:54:22.471 --> 00:54:24.450
So, in particular we
want to be able to solve

00:54:24.451 --> 00:54:28.345
this new broader objective
in the federated setting.

00:54:28.346 --> 00:54:29.920
If we're looking
at this objective,

00:54:29.921 --> 00:54:31.710
one thing that's
nice is that if you

00:54:31.711 --> 00:54:33.490
solve for these two terms,

00:54:33.491 --> 00:54:37.060
competing terms w and Omega
in an alternating fashion,

00:54:37.061 --> 00:54:39.555
Omega is in fact
quite easy to solve.

00:54:39.556 --> 00:54:41.180
You can solve that
just with the models

00:54:41.181 --> 00:54:43.940
w but without access to
any of the federated data,

00:54:43.941 --> 00:54:45.400
but w is what needs to

00:54:45.401 --> 00:54:47.735
be solved in
the federated setting.

00:54:47.736 --> 00:54:49.645
So the key question was,

00:54:49.646 --> 00:54:52.714
how can we extend
this update to W

00:54:52.715 --> 00:54:55.000
to work with a federated CoCoA or

00:54:55.001 --> 00:54:57.890
method that we had
described from before?

00:54:59.080 --> 00:55:06.480
Okay. I missed the key idea
of how we actually form

00:55:06.481 --> 00:55:07.670
these sub-problems when I was

00:55:07.671 --> 00:55:09.540
describing them earlier
and this is really

00:55:09.541 --> 00:55:11.250
important if you want to
think about extending

00:55:11.251 --> 00:55:13.305
it to another type of objective.

00:55:13.306 --> 00:55:14.710
So, recall that

00:55:14.711 --> 00:55:16.860
the way that federate CoCoA
and CoCoA work is that

00:55:16.861 --> 00:55:20.355
basically you have
some global problem of interest,

00:55:20.356 --> 00:55:22.520
the way that you actually
solve this global problem

00:55:22.521 --> 00:55:24.050
is by splitting
into sub-problems,

00:55:24.051 --> 00:55:26.140
one for each device.

00:55:26.630 --> 00:55:31.315
So the way that this division
is happening is

00:55:31.316 --> 00:55:32.830
actually very closely tied to

00:55:32.831 --> 00:55:35.540
the mathematical formulation
of the objective function.

00:55:35.541 --> 00:55:38.420
So in particular, what we
do in CoCoA, which is nice,

00:55:38.421 --> 00:55:40.460
is that the primal problem

00:55:40.461 --> 00:55:43.550
here is a bit difficult to solve.

00:55:43.551 --> 00:55:44.910
It's a bit difficult
to distribute

00:55:44.911 --> 00:55:46.859
this into separate sub-problems.

00:55:46.860 --> 00:55:49.230
Because you have data points
that are touching each of

00:55:49.231 --> 00:55:51.900
the models of interests,

00:55:51.901 --> 00:55:53.880
w. So what you can do is

00:55:53.881 --> 00:55:56.035
instead of looking at
this primal formulation,

00:55:56.036 --> 00:55:57.310
you can say consider the dual.

00:55:57.311 --> 00:55:58.860
So this is a very common trick

00:55:58.861 --> 00:56:01.005
that's often used
in optimization.

00:56:01.006 --> 00:56:03.040
Because this problem is convex,

00:56:03.041 --> 00:56:04.310
the dual will still arrive at

00:56:04.311 --> 00:56:07.300
the same solution as the primal.

00:56:07.301 --> 00:56:08.940
But this dual is actually

00:56:08.941 --> 00:56:10.320
much easier to
separate because you

00:56:10.321 --> 00:56:12.875
have one dual variable
per data point,

00:56:12.876 --> 00:56:14.060
and so this separates much more

00:56:14.061 --> 00:56:15.925
nicely across the machines.

00:56:15.926 --> 00:56:18.670
Okay. So, what I'm saying is

00:56:18.671 --> 00:56:20.980
mostly true except for
this regularization term

00:56:20.981 --> 00:56:23.110
here and the way that
we handle that in

00:56:23.111 --> 00:56:26.315
CoCoA is by doing a quadratic
approximation to this term.

00:56:26.316 --> 00:56:27.660
So essentially
what's happening is

00:56:27.661 --> 00:56:29.085
that at each iteration,

00:56:29.086 --> 00:56:31.310
we're computing
a quadratic approximation

00:56:31.311 --> 00:56:33.790
to the true objective
in the dual.

00:56:33.791 --> 00:56:37.840
Okay. So that's the secret sauce
in the back of how we're

00:56:37.841 --> 00:56:39.500
actually taking this problem

00:56:39.501 --> 00:56:41.410
and distributing it
across the devices,

00:56:41.411 --> 00:56:43.330
but it's important
to consider this

00:56:43.331 --> 00:56:45.460
because then
the key challenge is,

00:56:45.461 --> 00:56:48.070
how can we take the same idea
of using the primal and the

00:56:48.071 --> 00:56:52.175
dual and apply that to this
multitask learning objective?

00:56:52.176 --> 00:56:56.445
Okay. I'll just summarize
this quickly by saying,

00:56:56.446 --> 00:56:58.360
basically we can do

00:56:58.361 --> 00:57:00.650
something very similar
where the dual of interest

00:57:00.651 --> 00:57:03.690
looks like this and we can
define a sub-problem very

00:57:03.691 --> 00:57:07.270
similar to what we had done
in CoCoA and federated CoCoA,

00:57:07.271 --> 00:57:09.050
where basically we're looking at

00:57:09.051 --> 00:57:10.290
this dual and forming a

00:57:10.291 --> 00:57:13.410
quadratic approximation
around it.

00:57:13.790 --> 00:57:18.985
Okay. So putting these all
together just to recap.

00:57:18.986 --> 00:57:22.160
This was nice because
we addressed some of

00:57:22.161 --> 00:57:23.970
these key challenges in
the federated setting

00:57:23.971 --> 00:57:26.480
that really differ from
the data center setting.

00:57:26.481 --> 00:57:29.230
In federated CoCoA,
we handle things

00:57:29.231 --> 00:57:32.370
like expensive
communication by taking

00:57:32.371 --> 00:57:33.510
the problem and putting it into

00:57:33.511 --> 00:57:35.500
the sub-problems that are
solved to approximate

00:57:35.501 --> 00:57:37.220
accuracies and we allowed

00:57:37.221 --> 00:57:38.940
these approximate
accuracies to be

00:57:38.941 --> 00:57:40.580
highly variable which helps

00:57:40.581 --> 00:57:42.865
to deal with
systems heterogeneity.

00:57:42.866 --> 00:57:45.590
Then with MOCHA,
we also looked at

00:57:45.591 --> 00:57:48.390
the statistical issue of
personalization and we

00:57:48.391 --> 00:57:50.950
show that we can take
that model and also

00:57:50.951 --> 00:57:54.615
apply this similar ideas
as in Federated CoCoA.

00:57:54.616 --> 00:57:57.550
So, end with two quick slides

00:57:57.551 --> 00:57:59.685
in terms of where
this is going next.

00:57:59.686 --> 00:58:01.445
So, I'll note that
there are a lot of

00:58:01.446 --> 00:58:03.750
other exciting areas and

00:58:03.751 --> 00:58:06.790
open research opportunities
in federated learning.

00:58:06.791 --> 00:58:08.670
So, one is that the problems I

00:58:08.671 --> 00:58:10.475
talked about today
were all convex.

00:58:10.476 --> 00:58:12.200
Deep learning is also, of course,

00:58:12.201 --> 00:58:14.225
very popular and is non-convex.

00:58:14.226 --> 00:58:16.510
So there are interesting
opportunities and thinking about

00:58:16.511 --> 00:58:19.245
how to extend this for
non-convex setting.

00:58:19.246 --> 00:58:23.280
There are a lot of
interesting open problems

00:58:23.281 --> 00:58:24.890
to deal with privacy and security

00:58:24.891 --> 00:58:26.870
around the solution like this.

00:58:26.871 --> 00:58:30.220
So certainly federated learning
improves upon sending

00:58:30.221 --> 00:58:32.040
just the raw data but those model

00:58:32.041 --> 00:58:35.785
updates you also want to make
sure that those are secure.

00:58:35.786 --> 00:58:37.980
We talked about personalization

00:58:37.981 --> 00:58:39.150
through multitask learning.

00:58:39.151 --> 00:58:40.170
There are other ways to provide

00:58:40.171 --> 00:58:43.090
this like transfer learning
or model fine-tuning.

00:58:43.091 --> 00:58:45.830
We reduce communication by making

00:58:45.831 --> 00:58:48.240
this accuracy parameter
very flexible.

00:58:48.241 --> 00:58:50.215
There are other ways to
reduce this even further,

00:58:50.216 --> 00:58:52.695
things like compression
or lazy aggregation.

00:58:52.696 --> 00:58:55.165
Finally, in terms of
the machine-learning pipeline,

00:58:55.166 --> 00:58:57.445
I've been focusing on
the training portion.

00:58:57.446 --> 00:58:58.980
There are of course other ways,

00:58:58.981 --> 00:59:01.250
you could think about addressing

00:59:01.251 --> 00:59:03.615
the machine-learning pipeline
in this edge scenario.

00:59:03.616 --> 00:59:04.990
Things like doing on device

00:59:04.991 --> 00:59:07.020
feature extraction or
on device inference.

00:59:07.021 --> 00:59:08.770
So, there's a lot of

00:59:08.771 --> 00:59:12.835
open and exciting research
directions within this area.

00:59:12.836 --> 00:59:15.820
Okay. In terms of
my own research,

00:59:15.821 --> 00:59:17.440
I'll note that one thing

00:59:17.441 --> 00:59:19.470
I'm excited about is you
may have noticed some of

00:59:19.471 --> 00:59:21.470
the federated datasets that
we were looking at were very

00:59:21.471 --> 00:59:23.730
small and so we've

00:59:23.731 --> 00:59:25.020
been working with

00:59:25.021 --> 00:59:26.610
some collaboratives
to develop something

00:59:26.611 --> 00:59:29.885
called LEAF which is
a benchmark for federated data.

00:59:29.886 --> 00:59:31.670
It will contain a suite of

00:59:31.671 --> 00:59:33.470
open source datasets and

00:59:33.471 --> 00:59:35.670
evaluation framework
to help frame some of

00:59:35.671 --> 00:59:38.805
the statistical and systems
metrics and questions

00:59:38.806 --> 00:59:43.210
and it will be online very soon.

00:59:43.211 --> 00:59:45.460
So, please check back
on my website because

00:59:45.461 --> 00:59:48.720
we'll be releasing in
the next few months.

00:59:48.810 --> 00:59:53.080
Sorry, last thing, so
there's been a lot of

00:59:53.081 --> 00:59:54.880
exciting work around
systems and machine

00:59:54.881 --> 00:59:56.390
learning obviously at the summit

00:59:56.391 --> 00:59:58.410
today and within this session.

00:59:58.411 --> 01:00:02.140
I myself, I'm serving
as program chair for

01:00:02.141 --> 01:00:03.700
a new conference on systems

01:00:03.701 --> 01:00:05.660
and machine learning
called SysML.

01:00:05.661 --> 01:00:07.210
So, SysML, the goal of

01:00:07.211 --> 01:00:09.580
the conference is to elicit
new connections between

01:00:09.581 --> 01:00:13.010
the two fields and to make
sure that these discussions

01:00:13.011 --> 01:00:14.300
are happening not siloed within

01:00:14.301 --> 01:00:15.350
their own research communities

01:00:15.351 --> 01:00:16.980
but in a collaborative way.

01:00:16.981 --> 01:00:19.190
So, this last year,

01:00:19.191 --> 01:00:20.970
we held a one-day conference with

01:00:20.971 --> 01:00:23.794
over 300 attendees
and 200 submissions.

01:00:23.795 --> 01:00:26.410
This next year 2019 will be

01:00:26.411 --> 01:00:30.530
a full conference three days
with published proceedings.

01:00:30.531 --> 01:00:33.440
So if you're interested in
that, please take a look.

01:00:33.441 --> 01:00:36.005
The submissions are due
at the end of September

01:00:36.006 --> 01:00:38.030
and the conference
itself will be in

01:00:38.031 --> 01:00:44.390
Stanford in the end of March
through April of 2019.

01:00:44.760 --> 01:00:47.470
So, I think that was
all I had and I'd

01:00:47.471 --> 01:00:51.020
be happy to answer questions.

01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:07.470
So, I had a question
about the privacy aspect

01:01:07.471 --> 01:01:10.920
of the Federated approach.

01:01:10.921 --> 01:01:13.235
I mean, it seems to me that,

01:01:13.236 --> 01:01:16.875
if I'm worried about
the privacy of my data,

01:01:16.876 --> 01:01:19.675
then it should only
be used locally,

01:01:19.676 --> 01:01:21.130
and especially if
it's going to be

01:01:21.131 --> 01:01:23.795
for our personalized model,

01:01:23.796 --> 01:01:26.775
and not be shared at
all with the Cloud.

01:01:26.776 --> 01:01:28.605
But sending model updates,

01:01:28.606 --> 01:01:31.330
it seems to kind of like
open a whole kind of worms,

01:01:31.331 --> 01:01:33.840
with like model inference
attacks and things like this,

01:01:33.841 --> 01:01:35.400
but it's not really clear,
you're not going to be

01:01:35.401 --> 01:01:37.240
leaking basically raw data.

01:01:37.241 --> 01:01:38.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's
a great point. I think

01:01:38.791 --> 01:01:39.955
there's a trade-off there.

01:01:39.956 --> 01:01:42.995
If privacy is the most
important thing to you,

01:01:42.996 --> 01:01:44.440
more important perhaps than

01:01:44.441 --> 01:01:46.210
the accuracy that you're getting,

01:01:46.211 --> 01:01:47.590
then certainly it makes sense to

01:01:47.591 --> 01:01:49.345
keep that data completely local.

01:01:49.346 --> 01:01:53.320
The main thing that
the Federated setup is positing,

01:01:53.321 --> 01:01:55.180
is basically that sending

01:01:55.181 --> 01:01:57.290
model updates is
a better alternative,

01:01:57.291 --> 01:01:58.630
than sending the raw data.

01:01:58.631 --> 01:02:00.500
Which is what's frequently done

01:02:00.501 --> 01:02:02.500
in an a Cloud-based situation,

01:02:02.501 --> 01:02:03.730
where you have the
model in the Cloud,

01:02:03.731 --> 01:02:06.050
you're sending the raw data
up to get predictions.

01:02:06.051 --> 01:02:07.670
So, I agree with you that,

01:02:07.671 --> 01:02:11.305
the model updates themselves
could also be an issue.

01:02:11.306 --> 01:02:13.710
So, there's some
interesting work looking

01:02:13.711 --> 01:02:16.270
at combining that with
differential privacy,

01:02:16.271 --> 01:02:18.965
to ensure that the
model updates that you send,

01:02:18.966 --> 01:02:20.905
are in fact private.

01:02:20.906 --> 01:02:22.900
But, I think that
there's a trade-off,

01:02:22.901 --> 01:02:25.270
because if you keep
all the data completely local,

01:02:25.271 --> 01:02:26.260
then you're losing some of

01:02:26.261 --> 01:02:28.900
the interesting
information and data,

01:02:28.901 --> 01:02:31.080
that you could get from
devices like yours.

01:02:31.081 --> 01:02:32.820
So, yeah.

01:02:32.821 --> 01:02:40.400
&gt;&gt; I have a question about
the personalization.

01:02:40.401 --> 01:02:41.965
So I'm not a machine
learning person,

01:02:41.966 --> 01:02:44.180
but can you give some examples?

01:02:44.181 --> 01:02:46.020
I'm kind of trying to

01:02:46.021 --> 01:02:49.160
understand what the limitations
of it is, in terms of,

01:02:49.161 --> 01:02:53.280
is this about different phones

01:02:53.281 --> 01:02:54.855
having different types
of sensors,

01:02:54.856 --> 01:02:57.945
or people having
different preferences?

01:02:57.946 --> 01:03:00.100
&gt;&gt; Yes, I think
the example I gave

01:03:00.101 --> 01:03:02.500
before was that- for example,

01:03:02.501 --> 01:03:04.150
if you're trying to train

01:03:04.151 --> 01:03:06.640
a model for voice
recognition on your phone.

01:03:06.641 --> 01:03:08.530
If you train a single model for

01:03:08.531 --> 01:03:11.530
voice recognition across
all voices, it could be that,

01:03:11.531 --> 01:03:12.770
that doesn't perform as well,

01:03:12.771 --> 01:03:14.210
as one that's personalized to

01:03:14.211 --> 01:03:17.075
the specific voice of
a specific person.

01:03:17.076 --> 01:03:19.610
So, certainly there are
some aspects of speech

01:03:19.611 --> 01:03:22.020
that are going to be the
same across all people,

01:03:22.021 --> 01:03:24.310
but you could also imagine
that certain aspects of

01:03:24.311 --> 01:03:27.040
the way that a person
speaks or their accent,

01:03:27.041 --> 01:03:29.370
also influence
speech recognition.

01:03:29.371 --> 01:03:30.840
So, that's the trade off,

01:03:30.841 --> 01:03:32.490
where you want to do
some global modelling,

01:03:32.491 --> 01:03:36.370
but also have some amount
of it that is local or

01:03:36.371 --> 01:03:43.345
personal to the data. Thanks.

01:03:43.346 --> 01:03:47.140
&gt;&gt; Thank you very
much. Then, we're

01:03:47.141 --> 01:03:49.250
going to have our next speaker.

01:03:56.400 --> 01:03:59.750
I am very excited to
introduce Behnaz Arzani.

01:03:59.751 --> 01:04:02.290
She is from a MSR as well.

01:04:02.291 --> 01:04:05.670
She's going to tell us about
Self-managing networks,

01:04:05.671 --> 01:04:06.690
and hopefully will not have

01:04:06.691 --> 01:04:09.260
any networking
problems over again.

01:04:11.990 --> 01:04:14.865
Except we have A/V problems.

01:04:14.866 --> 01:04:17.730
&gt;&gt; Is this doesn't
work, it seems like.

01:04:17.731 --> 01:04:19.785
&gt;&gt; You have to-

01:04:19.786 --> 01:04:21.860
&gt;&gt; I did.

01:04:21.861 --> 01:04:24.260
&gt;&gt; What do we have to do?

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:28.270
&gt;&gt; I have one in
my bag, I can use mine.

01:04:28.271 --> 01:04:34.750
Let's see. I can use my own too.

01:04:34.751 --> 01:04:36.535
Let me Google.

01:04:36.536 --> 01:04:38.905
Actually, this is recognizing
it. Let me grab mine.

01:04:38.906 --> 01:04:44.180
This is in bag.

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:47.305
My bag isn't there.

01:04:47.306 --> 01:04:52.520
Okay, I can just use mine.

01:04:54.330 --> 01:04:57.685
&gt;&gt; Okay. Sorry everyone.

01:04:57.686 --> 01:04:59.930
So, today I'm gonna
be talking about,

01:04:59.931 --> 01:05:01.810
this as more of a
networking talk.

01:05:01.811 --> 01:05:03.190
I'm not really
a machine learning person,

01:05:03.191 --> 01:05:05.325
but what I want to talk about is,

01:05:05.326 --> 01:05:07.780
how we move towards networking
to manage themselves.

01:05:07.781 --> 01:05:09.060
What I want to do is,

01:05:09.061 --> 01:05:11.380
sort of talk about what I
learned through my research,

01:05:11.381 --> 01:05:12.530
about where machine learning can

01:05:12.531 --> 01:05:15.824
actually help in networking.

01:05:15.825 --> 01:05:19.570
So, the picture I've here
is of Albert Greenberg.

01:05:19.571 --> 01:05:22.880
He is the head of
Azure networking.

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:26.140
He has 1,029 reports,

01:05:26.141 --> 01:05:27.700
and if you look at
what these people

01:05:27.701 --> 01:05:29.855
do on a day-to-day basis,

01:05:29.856 --> 01:05:32.320
a lot of their time is spent on

01:05:32.321 --> 01:05:35.815
managing and
configuring networks.

01:05:35.816 --> 01:05:40.965
If you think about when
networking as a field started,

01:05:40.966 --> 01:05:44.675
the first paper on
networking was a 1973.

01:05:44.676 --> 01:05:48.145
SIGCOMM started in 1969.

01:05:48.146 --> 01:05:51.760
SIGMETRICS has been
around since 1971.

01:05:51.761 --> 01:05:54.140
Then NSDI started in 2004,

01:05:54.141 --> 01:05:56.510
and yet we still need people to

01:05:56.511 --> 01:05:59.589
actually actively configure
and manage your networks.

01:05:59.590 --> 01:06:01.680
So, what I wanted to do,

01:06:01.681 --> 01:06:02.930
is to try to understand,

01:06:02.931 --> 01:06:04.495
"Why do we still need this?"

01:06:04.496 --> 01:06:06.720
Like, why are people
spending so many hours,

01:06:06.721 --> 01:06:10.295
and so much money, just on
configuration and management.

01:06:10.296 --> 01:06:12.880
So, what I'll do, is I'll
talk about what my research

01:06:12.881 --> 01:06:15.660
and collaboration with Azure
over the last three years.

01:06:15.661 --> 01:06:17.705
What I'll describe is,

01:06:17.706 --> 01:06:20.130
what I've learned to be
the cause of this problem,

01:06:20.131 --> 01:06:21.260
and where machine
learning can come

01:06:21.261 --> 01:06:23.155
into play to help solve it.

01:06:23.156 --> 01:06:26.910
This has a couple of
different aspects.

01:06:26.911 --> 01:06:28.480
One aspect is diagnosis,

01:06:28.481 --> 01:06:30.695
the other aspect is security.

01:06:30.696 --> 01:06:32.800
Then I'll talk about, what
are the common themes,

01:06:32.801 --> 01:06:35.430
and how can actually
I help with it.

01:06:35.431 --> 01:06:37.720
So, I borrowed this slide
from Amin Vahdat,

01:06:37.721 --> 01:06:41.290
who's basically the counterpart
of Albert in Google.

01:06:41.291 --> 01:06:43.730
According to him, availability is

01:06:43.731 --> 01:06:46.920
the main foundation on which
our datacenters are built.

01:06:46.921 --> 01:06:49.420
So, we want them
to be manageable.

01:06:49.421 --> 01:06:50.935
We want them to be performant,

01:06:50.936 --> 01:06:51.980
but if they're not available,

01:06:51.981 --> 01:06:54.330
we did get none of
that benefit anyway.

01:06:54.331 --> 01:06:56.240
So, the first thing that can

01:06:56.241 --> 01:06:58.135
disrupt that, is
going to be failures.

01:06:58.136 --> 01:07:01.415
Failures are going to
hurt our availability.

01:07:01.416 --> 01:07:05.885
To give an example, why it
is a diagnosis is hard,

01:07:05.886 --> 01:07:07.195
is think of this scenario.

01:07:07.196 --> 01:07:08.570
You are a researcher,

01:07:08.571 --> 01:07:09.880
you want to use a Cloud.

01:07:09.881 --> 01:07:12.360
You pull out the Cloud VM,

01:07:12.361 --> 01:07:13.940
you go over the Cloud network and

01:07:13.941 --> 01:07:15.815
then you connect to some service.

01:07:15.816 --> 01:07:18.145
So, when something goes down,

01:07:18.146 --> 01:07:21.330
if you imagine that the network
is is managed by Azure,

01:07:21.331 --> 01:07:23.070
and then the service could be

01:07:23.071 --> 01:07:25.035
managed by some other company,

01:07:25.036 --> 01:07:27.510
the problem that this causes is,

01:07:27.511 --> 01:07:29.044
when you have a failure,

01:07:29.045 --> 01:07:31.225
you can have one
of two situations.

01:07:31.226 --> 01:07:34.830
Either, someone accepts
responsibility for the failure.

01:07:34.831 --> 01:07:36.570
So, either network operator

01:07:36.571 --> 01:07:38.195
looks at their networks
and figures out,

01:07:38.196 --> 01:07:40.085
"Oh, I know why this happens."

01:07:40.086 --> 01:07:41.920
Or, on the other hand,

01:07:41.921 --> 01:07:43.620
the service operator
looks at their system,

01:07:43.621 --> 01:07:45.275
and finds the cause
of the problem.

01:07:45.276 --> 01:07:47.270
In that situation,
you can eventually

01:07:47.271 --> 01:07:49.205
figure out what was
the root cause and solve it.

01:07:49.206 --> 01:07:53.300
But often, you run into
this particular situation,

01:07:53.301 --> 01:07:54.940
where the network starts
blaming the service.

01:07:54.941 --> 01:07:56.140
The service blames the network,

01:07:56.141 --> 01:07:59.680
and nobody really wants to
take responsibility for this.

01:07:59.970 --> 01:08:02.190
We had a real example of

01:08:02.191 --> 01:08:03.690
this happening in
Azure networking,

01:08:03.691 --> 01:08:06.910
where we had VMs
that were connecting

01:08:06.911 --> 01:08:10.490
to virtual storage
panic and reboot,

01:08:10.491 --> 01:08:12.895
because their connections
to storage were failing.

01:08:12.896 --> 01:08:15.730
In these situations,
we had tickets created

01:08:15.731 --> 01:08:18.505
that were basically showing
customers are being impacted,

01:08:18.506 --> 01:08:21.050
but nobody was willing to
take responsibility for it.

01:08:21.051 --> 01:08:22.610
So, storage was blaming network,

01:08:22.611 --> 01:08:23.875
network was blaming storage,

01:08:23.876 --> 01:08:25.550
and the problem was
not getting resolved.

01:08:25.551 --> 01:08:27.640
So, the question was,

01:08:27.641 --> 01:08:31.985
how can we actually attribute
blame to the right team?

01:08:31.986 --> 01:08:34.060
So, we created a system that

01:08:34.061 --> 01:08:36.300
was published in SIGCOMM 2016,

01:08:36.301 --> 01:08:38.390
we call it NetPoirot.

01:08:38.391 --> 01:08:41.120
The main requirement for it was,

01:08:41.121 --> 01:08:42.990
that it didn't require
anything to be

01:08:42.991 --> 01:08:45.570
installed in the
network or storage,

01:08:45.571 --> 01:08:47.075
because neither one
of them wanting

01:08:47.076 --> 01:08:48.365
to have that overhead,

01:08:48.366 --> 01:08:50.150
but we'll still be
able to say who is

01:08:50.151 --> 01:08:53.600
responsible for the failure
when the failure happened.

01:08:53.700 --> 01:08:55.930
What we decided to do,

01:08:55.931 --> 01:08:59.095
is take advantage of
a network protocol called TCP.

01:08:59.096 --> 01:09:01.440
Now, TCP is designed

01:09:01.441 --> 01:09:03.465
to be able to detect
network failures.

01:09:03.466 --> 01:09:06.410
It is designed to be able
to give you reliability,

01:09:06.411 --> 01:09:09.360
when you're going over
a best effort distributed

01:09:09.361 --> 01:09:10.770
system that is not guaranteed

01:09:10.771 --> 01:09:12.500
to deliver all of your packets.

01:09:12.501 --> 01:09:13.920
But, in our theory was,

01:09:13.921 --> 01:09:16.100
if it's designed to detect
failures in the network,

01:09:16.101 --> 01:09:18.880
can it distinguish between
failures in the network,

01:09:18.881 --> 01:09:22.630
and failures on the server side?
So, we took a bet.

01:09:22.631 --> 01:09:25.905
What we did was, we
designed the system over,

01:09:25.906 --> 01:09:28.700
that looked at TCP statistics
we gathered from the host,

01:09:28.701 --> 01:09:30.220
so it doesn't gather
any data from

01:09:30.221 --> 01:09:32.170
the network or the service,

01:09:32.171 --> 01:09:33.905
and try to identify if we have

01:09:33.906 --> 01:09:37.295
a client-side failure or
server-side or network failure.

01:09:37.296 --> 01:09:39.140
So, the way this system worked,

01:09:39.141 --> 01:09:42.060
is we had a monitoring
agent that was deployed on

01:09:42.061 --> 01:09:45.685
all hosts across Azure
or Azure datacenters.

01:09:45.686 --> 01:09:48.420
We injected failures
onto a subset of

01:09:48.421 --> 01:09:51.470
these machines to look at
what failures look like,

01:09:51.471 --> 01:09:53.685
in terms of TCP statistics.

01:09:53.686 --> 01:09:56.815
Then we trained
a simple Random Forest model

01:09:56.816 --> 01:09:58.040
using this data,

01:09:58.041 --> 01:09:59.860
to see if we could
actually distinguish

01:09:59.861 --> 01:10:01.600
what was causing the problem.

01:10:01.601 --> 01:10:04.040
Then this model was running
on each of the host

01:10:04.041 --> 01:10:06.715
to figure out why
the VM is rebooting.

01:10:06.716 --> 01:10:09.650
It turned out to work
really, really well.

01:10:09.651 --> 01:10:12.050
So, while we solve that problem,

01:10:12.051 --> 01:10:14.350
we still have one more,
which was, okay, "Now,

01:10:14.351 --> 01:10:15.530
I know that I have

01:10:15.531 --> 01:10:17.090
failures in my network,"
and it turned out

01:10:17.091 --> 01:10:18.410
actually 20 percent of the time

01:10:18.411 --> 01:10:20.670
those failures were
in the network.

01:10:20.671 --> 01:10:24.240
Then we ran into in
separate situation where,

01:10:24.241 --> 01:10:26.060
you could have multiple links in

01:10:26.061 --> 01:10:27.875
your network having failed,

01:10:27.876 --> 01:10:30.805
or be dropping packets at
any given point in time.

01:10:30.806 --> 01:10:33.710
You can actually have a link
with a high drop rate,

01:10:33.711 --> 01:10:35.860
and link with a slightly
lower drop rate.

01:10:35.861 --> 01:10:37.965
But, you care about
one of them more.

01:10:37.966 --> 01:10:40.690
Because while the link
with high drop rate is

01:10:40.691 --> 01:10:43.600
dropping a lot of packets is
not impacting any customers,

01:10:43.601 --> 01:10:46.405
or the customer is not noticing
that packet drop rate,

01:10:46.406 --> 01:10:49.090
versus this other
lower drop rate packet

01:10:49.091 --> 01:10:51.280
is impacting customer
whose application is very,

01:10:51.281 --> 01:10:54.130
very sensitive to
this type of failure.

01:10:54.131 --> 01:10:56.710
So, what we wanted
to be able to do,

01:10:56.711 --> 01:10:58.180
is associate failed links

01:10:58.181 --> 01:10:59.630
with the problems
that they're causing.

01:10:59.631 --> 01:11:00.800
So, essentially,
we wanted to say,

01:11:00.801 --> 01:11:03.540
this customer is impacted
by that link failure.

01:11:03.541 --> 01:11:06.485
This customer is impacted
by this link failure.

01:11:06.486 --> 01:11:08.960
Now, here we actually didn't
use machine learning,

01:11:08.961 --> 01:11:11.025
but I'll talk about how we
actually solve the problem.

01:11:11.026 --> 01:11:12.435
But, before I do that,

01:11:12.436 --> 01:11:14.605
I'll talk about why
it's actually hard.

01:11:14.606 --> 01:11:17.520
So, in an ideal situation,
what you want to do is say,

01:11:17.521 --> 01:11:19.200
this is the connections
that this guy has,

01:11:19.201 --> 01:11:21.465
these are the connections
that this guy has.

01:11:21.466 --> 01:11:22.880
If I know where my failures are,

01:11:22.881 --> 01:11:24.140
I can basically map

01:11:24.141 --> 01:11:25.340
the failures of the connection

01:11:25.341 --> 01:11:27.470
and then solve the problem.

01:11:27.471 --> 01:11:30.020
But the problem is
operators don't always

01:11:30.021 --> 01:11:32.230
know where the failed links
are either, right?

01:11:32.231 --> 01:11:33.760
If you think about the scale

01:11:33.761 --> 01:11:35.375
in which datacenters operate,

01:11:35.376 --> 01:11:37.310
this is becomes
an extremely hard problem,

01:11:37.311 --> 01:11:40.400
is basically like finding
a needle in a haystack.

01:11:40.680 --> 01:11:42.775
The thing is, we also

01:11:42.776 --> 01:11:44.290
have packet drop rates
that are obeyed

01:11:44.291 --> 01:11:45.470
because of the normal

01:11:45.471 --> 01:11:47.330
day-to-day operations
of the network.

01:11:47.331 --> 01:11:49.400
So tail drop rates,

01:11:49.401 --> 01:11:51.840
detecting them doesn't
really give you anything.

01:11:51.841 --> 01:11:54.780
You can't take any action
on those types of problems.

01:11:54.781 --> 01:11:57.760
So, one-off, transient
drops do occur on

01:11:57.761 --> 01:12:01.970
many links and they add noise
to the diagnosis problem.

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:06.260
So, the requirements that
we were asked when we were

01:12:06.261 --> 01:12:07.770
solving this problem was to be

01:12:07.771 --> 01:12:09.600
able to detect
short-lived failures,

01:12:09.601 --> 01:12:12.350
to be able to detect
concurrent failures and we

01:12:12.351 --> 01:12:15.165
wanted the approach to
be robust to noise.

01:12:15.166 --> 01:12:16.780
But again like
the previous approach,

01:12:16.781 --> 01:12:18.290
we again had this mandate from

01:12:18.291 --> 01:12:20.080
the network operators
where we were

01:12:20.081 --> 01:12:21.790
not allowed to deploy anything on

01:12:21.791 --> 01:12:23.850
the servers or anything
on the network.

01:12:23.851 --> 01:12:26.260
So we had to again
use an approach that

01:12:26.261 --> 01:12:29.810
was only on the client side.

01:12:29.811 --> 01:12:33.460
So, the strawman solution
to this would have been

01:12:33.461 --> 01:12:34.740
something that is traditionally

01:12:34.741 --> 01:12:36.960
networking known as tomography.

01:12:36.961 --> 01:12:40.060
The way it works in
this example is the following.

01:12:40.061 --> 01:12:42.440
If you have a simple topology
where the link between 4

01:12:42.441 --> 01:12:45.160
and 2 is bad and you
have three connections,

01:12:45.161 --> 01:12:47.400
you can basically
assign a variable.

01:12:47.401 --> 01:12:48.870
Let's assume you know
the path of each of

01:12:48.871 --> 01:12:50.730
these individual connections
for now, right?

01:12:50.731 --> 01:12:52.110
You can assign
a variable to each of

01:12:52.111 --> 01:12:55.325
the links and you can form
a system of equations

01:12:55.326 --> 01:12:58.410
that equate 0 if there's

01:12:58.411 --> 01:13:00.260
no fail link along
the path and equate

01:13:00.261 --> 01:13:02.950
1 if you have a fail link
along your path.

01:13:02.951 --> 01:13:04.910
By solving this
system of equations,

01:13:04.911 --> 01:13:06.250
you can basically
figure out which

01:13:06.251 --> 01:13:07.650
links in your network
have failed.

01:13:07.651 --> 01:13:10.060
So, the easiest reason

01:13:10.061 --> 01:13:11.760
why this fails is that
you don't always have

01:13:11.761 --> 01:13:13.790
enough flows to be able to have

01:13:13.791 --> 01:13:16.165
a full solvable set of equations.

01:13:16.166 --> 01:13:18.220
There are more problems beyond

01:13:18.221 --> 01:13:19.470
this where we're now
working standpoint

01:13:19.471 --> 01:13:20.420
in the sense that it's really

01:13:20.421 --> 01:13:22.450
hard to have a path of
every single flow in

01:13:22.451 --> 01:13:24.790
your network and a lot
of other things.

01:13:24.791 --> 01:13:27.460
I won't go into the detail
of that to bore you,

01:13:27.461 --> 01:13:30.610
but we had a system we call
007 that was published in

01:13:30.611 --> 01:13:34.705
NSDI 2018 that basically
tries to solve this problem.

01:13:34.706 --> 01:13:36.760
I will just give you
a teaser of how it

01:13:36.761 --> 01:13:39.160
works and the way it works is we

01:13:39.161 --> 01:13:41.030
have again an agent
that's only on

01:13:41.031 --> 01:13:43.950
all hosts in Azure so
it's on the client side.

01:13:43.951 --> 01:13:47.635
It monitors TCP connections
running on these hosts.

01:13:47.636 --> 01:13:50.295
Every time there's a packet drop,

01:13:50.296 --> 01:13:53.250
this agent gets notified
that a packet drop

01:13:53.251 --> 01:13:56.100
happened and the host

01:13:56.101 --> 01:13:58.910
then vote on the status
of the links.

01:13:58.911 --> 01:14:02.170
So, the way the voting
works is in this case,

01:14:02.171 --> 01:14:04.620
this connection didn't
experience any failures,

01:14:04.621 --> 01:14:06.130
all links get a vote of 0,

01:14:06.131 --> 01:14:08.540
1 and 2 and we actually have

01:14:08.541 --> 01:14:11.810
a theoretical guarantee that
this is supposed to work,

01:14:11.811 --> 01:14:13.515
that is resilient to noise,

01:14:13.516 --> 01:14:16.565
all of the stuff that
operators care about.

01:14:16.566 --> 01:14:18.490
Now, in the context of security,

01:14:18.491 --> 01:14:19.610
I've also been looking now

01:14:19.611 --> 01:14:21.970
at a problem again in
collaboration with

01:14:21.971 --> 01:14:24.920
Azure networking and here this is

01:14:24.921 --> 01:14:26.270
a completely different flavor

01:14:26.271 --> 01:14:28.420
than what I was
talking about earlier.

01:14:28.421 --> 01:14:29.790
What we want to be able to

01:14:29.791 --> 01:14:32.475
do is to detect compromise VMs.

01:14:32.476 --> 01:14:35.700
It's a very simple problem
and if I have

01:14:35.701 --> 01:14:38.795
anti-malware on all of my VMs
in an ideal situation,

01:14:38.796 --> 01:14:40.130
I really don't have a problem

01:14:40.131 --> 01:14:42.430
anytime my VM gets
compromised the.

01:14:42.431 --> 01:14:45.500
Anti-malware should be able
to find it and I'm good.

01:14:45.501 --> 01:14:48.180
The problem is that in reality,

01:14:48.181 --> 01:14:49.970
this is what the
system looks like.

01:14:49.971 --> 01:14:52.010
A lot of customers
don't want to have

01:14:52.011 --> 01:14:55.185
a Microsoft owned
anti-malware software

01:14:55.186 --> 01:14:56.520
on their machines.

01:14:56.521 --> 01:14:59.020
So, a lot of these VMs
are not protected.

01:14:59.021 --> 01:15:00.685
So, then the question is

01:15:00.686 --> 01:15:02.740
how do I protect those VMs
because these VMs

01:15:02.741 --> 01:15:03.940
can now cause damage to

01:15:03.941 --> 01:15:06.830
other VMs that are running
in the same data center?

01:15:06.831 --> 01:15:08.800
The way we ended up deciding

01:15:08.801 --> 01:15:10.340
to do this was based on a theory.

01:15:10.341 --> 01:15:13.740
We thought that
if compromised VMs

01:15:13.741 --> 01:15:15.335
live in network footprint,

01:15:15.336 --> 01:15:16.840
then maybe the connection graph

01:15:16.841 --> 01:15:18.540
changes significantly enough when

01:15:18.541 --> 01:15:19.770
a VM is compromised from

01:15:19.771 --> 01:15:22.220
its past behavior for us
to be able to detect it.

01:15:22.221 --> 01:15:24.460
So, this is ongoing work,
but right now,

01:15:24.461 --> 01:15:26.400
the results seem
rather promising so we

01:15:26.401 --> 01:15:29.395
don't know if it
will work or not,

01:15:29.396 --> 01:15:33.880
but what I want to do
now that I've talked

01:15:33.881 --> 01:15:35.620
about the general scope
of the problems

01:15:35.621 --> 01:15:38.105
I've worked on is,

01:15:38.106 --> 01:15:39.885
sorry, one more slide.

01:15:39.886 --> 01:15:43.140
What are common themes across

01:15:43.141 --> 01:15:44.500
these problems and where

01:15:44.501 --> 01:15:46.965
machine learning
can actually help?

01:15:46.966 --> 01:15:50.165
So, before I go into that,

01:15:50.166 --> 01:15:53.140
I found this RFC for those of
you who work on networking,

01:15:53.141 --> 01:15:56.295
you know that RFCs are basically
standards in networking

01:15:56.296 --> 01:16:01.425
and they're basically
is from 1996.

01:16:01.426 --> 01:16:03.245
So, it's even before,

01:16:03.246 --> 01:16:05.340
I think I was five
at that point maybe,

01:16:05.341 --> 01:16:07.360
but I feel like this truths

01:16:07.361 --> 01:16:09.880
that are said in
that RFC still hold.

01:16:09.881 --> 01:16:12.555
So first of all, some things
in life can never

01:16:12.556 --> 01:16:14.250
be appreciated or nor

01:16:14.251 --> 01:16:16.060
understood until you
actually do it yourself.

01:16:16.061 --> 01:16:18.030
One of the things that I've
learned after three years

01:16:18.031 --> 01:16:20.255
of collaborating with
Azure networking has been,

01:16:20.256 --> 01:16:22.930
until you experience
the problems and if you

01:16:22.931 --> 01:16:25.770
don't have the domain knowledge,
it's going to be really,

01:16:25.771 --> 01:16:28.010
really hard to create
a solution that

01:16:28.011 --> 01:16:32.485
the operators actually want
to use and is practical.

01:16:32.486 --> 01:16:36.400
The second thing is
it's always possible to

01:16:36.401 --> 01:16:37.870
have multiple separate solutions

01:16:37.871 --> 01:16:40.095
to the problem and
try to combine them,

01:16:40.096 --> 01:16:41.990
but when you work with operators,

01:16:41.991 --> 01:16:43.850
one of the things that
you would notice is that

01:16:43.851 --> 01:16:46.085
they always prefer
the simplest solution.

01:16:46.086 --> 01:16:49.150
You can provide complex models
that work really,

01:16:49.151 --> 01:16:51.800
really well in theory,
but in practice,

01:16:51.801 --> 01:16:52.960
an operator should be able to

01:16:52.961 --> 01:16:55.575
verify why your model
is not working.

01:16:55.576 --> 01:16:57.815
So, if I'm an operator
and if I look at

01:16:57.816 --> 01:16:59.250
a number coming out of a system

01:16:59.251 --> 01:17:00.545
and I don't know what it means,

01:17:00.546 --> 01:17:03.230
if a system gives me a wrong
number at one point in time,

01:17:03.231 --> 01:17:04.420
I'm going to lose confidence

01:17:04.421 --> 01:17:05.500
in the system and
I'm never going to

01:17:05.501 --> 01:17:09.150
use it again and
that happens a lot.

01:17:09.250 --> 01:17:13.200
So, going back to the original
question I asked which was

01:17:13.201 --> 01:17:16.915
why do we need operators to
manage our networks today?

01:17:16.916 --> 01:17:18.590
There are a couple of reasons.

01:17:18.591 --> 01:17:21.290
First of all,
the ideal data that we

01:17:21.291 --> 01:17:22.890
need to figure out

01:17:22.891 --> 01:17:25.130
the cause of problems
is often missing.

01:17:25.131 --> 01:17:27.850
The monitoring we need
is expensive a lot of

01:17:27.851 --> 01:17:30.600
times and so one place that
machine learning can really,

01:17:30.601 --> 01:17:33.380
really help is
figuring out when and

01:17:33.381 --> 01:17:36.930
where to initiate monitoring
of what type of data.

01:17:36.931 --> 01:17:39.400
The other thing is gathering
the right data can be

01:17:39.401 --> 01:17:42.090
expensive so that's another place

01:17:42.091 --> 01:17:44.050
where I think we can contribute.

01:17:44.051 --> 01:17:46.370
Sometimes it's not even
clear what the right data

01:17:46.371 --> 01:17:48.790
is to figure out
the source of a subset of

01:17:48.791 --> 01:17:50.920
these problems and there's

01:17:50.921 --> 01:17:52.670
sometimes like the case
in network which

01:17:52.671 --> 01:17:54.320
we found is that
there is data that

01:17:54.321 --> 01:17:56.560
indirectly points to
the cause of the problem,

01:17:56.561 --> 01:18:00.390
and sure causality is important,

01:18:00.391 --> 01:18:01.790
but sometimes correlation is just

01:18:01.791 --> 01:18:04.570
enough and it's
all that you need.

01:18:05.130 --> 01:18:08.710
So, to sum it up,

01:18:08.711 --> 01:18:10.985
this is where I
think AI can help.

01:18:10.986 --> 01:18:13.580
If you can predict when a
failure is about to happen,

01:18:13.581 --> 01:18:15.660
we can always figure out
what monitoring to turn

01:18:15.661 --> 01:18:19.350
on and what that
monitoring should be.

01:18:19.351 --> 01:18:23.810
If you can identify where
the complex correlations are,

01:18:24.700 --> 01:18:27.070
sometimes it's the case that you

01:18:27.071 --> 01:18:29.424
can't avoid operator involvement.

01:18:29.425 --> 01:18:31.090
Sometimes operator is needed to

01:18:31.091 --> 01:18:32.750
solve the problem but
if you can identify

01:18:32.751 --> 01:18:34.545
when that help is actually needed

01:18:34.546 --> 01:18:36.850
and when the alert is noise,

01:18:36.851 --> 01:18:38.625
that would reduce pain

01:18:38.626 --> 01:18:42.400
in complex platforms
like Azure by alot.

01:18:42.401 --> 01:18:44.170
So, with that, I'll end with

01:18:44.171 --> 01:18:46.240
this other truth that I find is

01:18:46.241 --> 01:18:49.360
important to keep
in mind. Thanks.

01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:10.090
&gt;&gt; So there's a lot
of new work coming

01:19:10.091 --> 01:19:13.060
out in [inaudible] It's
a very hard topic in

01:19:13.061 --> 01:19:14.815
the machine learning area
that now working on

01:19:14.816 --> 01:19:16.210
interpreting the output of

01:19:16.211 --> 01:19:18.370
the models including
deep learning,

01:19:18.371 --> 01:19:22.685
sorry, whether deep learning
or otherwise like

01:19:22.686 --> 01:19:24.830
how do we interpret
especially like

01:19:24.831 --> 01:19:28.480
complex decision trees
and everything, right?

01:19:28.481 --> 01:19:33.260
Do you find that kind
of work still being

01:19:33.261 --> 01:19:35.260
useful or is still too far away

01:19:35.261 --> 01:19:38.260
from like really
helping this domain?

01:19:38.261 --> 01:19:40.680
&gt;&gt; There's two ways I
find this useful and

01:19:40.681 --> 01:19:42.825
one of them is
lightly idiotic and

01:19:42.826 --> 01:19:46.400
the one that I find it most
useful is when you want

01:19:46.401 --> 01:19:50.040
to build operator trust in
your system or in your model.

01:19:50.041 --> 01:19:53.020
It helps if they understand
why and how the model

01:19:53.021 --> 01:19:54.310
is working or when it's not

01:19:54.311 --> 01:19:56.115
working why isn't working, right?

01:19:56.116 --> 01:19:59.450
That is a place that I think
that's very much needed.

01:19:59.451 --> 01:20:02.410
I've seen a lot of systems
built by Azure networking

01:20:02.411 --> 01:20:04.450
themselves where the system

01:20:04.451 --> 01:20:05.970
is deployed, it's very useful.

01:20:05.971 --> 01:20:07.750
It makes a couple of mistakes
and people are like "Oh,

01:20:07.751 --> 01:20:09.670
the system is crap, we're
never going to use it again."

01:20:09.671 --> 01:20:12.220
That's not really true
but it's like people

01:20:12.221 --> 01:20:13.430
lost confidence in the system so

01:20:13.431 --> 01:20:15.560
they're never going to
use it again, right?

01:20:15.561 --> 01:20:18.480
So, that's one thing but
the other thing I think is

01:20:18.481 --> 01:20:20.700
sometimes like in the case
of the TCP stuff,

01:20:20.701 --> 01:20:22.790
the Neporo stuff that we did,

01:20:22.791 --> 01:20:25.700
you can gain a lot
of knowledge about

01:20:25.701 --> 01:20:28.550
the protocol itself or
about the fundamentals of

01:20:28.551 --> 01:20:30.280
the system itself from

01:20:30.281 --> 01:20:32.880
the explainability of the models
like what we learned was

01:20:32.881 --> 01:20:35.390
a lot about how TCP
reacts to failures on

01:20:35.391 --> 01:20:36.830
the server side that we

01:20:36.831 --> 01:20:38.880
didn't know before we
didn't expect to see.

01:20:38.881 --> 01:20:40.290
So, that was also something

01:20:40.291 --> 01:20:41.730
like and that's part
of the reason we

01:20:41.731 --> 01:20:43.320
use decision trees
because they give

01:20:43.321 --> 01:20:45.130
you explainability but yeah,

01:20:45.131 --> 01:20:48.400
so those are the things I
think where this can be used.

